(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-immigration"]={"plain":{"bills":{"immigration":{"slug":"immigration","title":"The Day in Court Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-101","heading":"\u00a7 101. Absolute protection for persons present in the United States","part":"TITLE I \u2014 PROTECTED EXPRESSION","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1227(a)(4)(C) by striking clause (ii) and inserting:*\n\n> **(ii) Protected expression.** An alien **who is physically present in the United States** shall not be deportable under clause (i) because of the alien's past, current, or expected beliefs, statements, or associations, **if such beliefs, statements, or associations would be lawful within the United States.**\n>\n> **(iii) No exception.** **The exception in section 1182(a)(3)(C)(iii) of this title does not apply to deportability under clause (i).** The protection in clause (ii) is absolute, and no determination by any officer, however denominated, renders a person deportable under clause (i) on a basis described in clause (ii).","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE I \u2014 PROTECTED EXPRESSION","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1182(a)(3)(C) and \u00a7 1227(a)(4)(C).*"}]},{"anchor":"sec-102","heading":"\u00a7 102. \"Solely\" struck from the officials exception","part":"TITLE I \u2014 PROTECTED EXPRESSION","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1182(a)(3)(C)(ii):*\n\n> \"...shall not be excludable or subject to restrictions or conditions on entry into the United States under clause (i) ~~solely~~ because of the alien's past, current, or expected beliefs, statements, or associations, if such beliefs, statements, or associations would be lawful within the United States.\""},{"anchor":"sec-103","heading":"\u00a7 103. The personal determination must have content","part":"TITLE I \u2014 PROTECTED EXPRESSION","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1182(a)(3)(C)(iii) by adding at the end:*\n\n> **A determination under this clause satisfies this clause only if the Secretary of State, in person:**\n>\n> **(1)** makes it **before** the alien is denied admission or subjected to any restriction or condition;\n>\n> **(2)** records it in a writing that identifies the alien, states **the compelling United States foreign policy interest** by name, and states **the facts particular to that alien** establishing that admission would compromise it;\n>\n> **(3)** states expressly that the determination **does not rest** on beliefs, statements, or associations that would be lawful within the United States, and **identifies the non-protected basis** on which it does rest; and\n>\n> **(4)** furnishes the writing to the alien and to the alien's counsel.\n>\n> **A determination reciting only the statutory conclusion does not satisfy this clause.** A writing prepared after the action does not satisfy it, and **no ground not stated in the writing may be relied upon** to sustain the action."},{"anchor":"sec-104","heading":"\u00a7 104. The notification duty carries a consequence","part":"TITLE I \u2014 PROTECTED EXPRESSION","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1182(a)(3)(C)(iv):*\n\n> \"If a determination is made under clause (iii) with respect to an alien, the Secretary of State must notify **not later than 15 days after the determination** the chairmen of the Committees on the Judiciary and Foreign Affairs of the House of Representatives and of the Committees on the Judiciary and Foreign Relations of the Senate of the identity of the alien and the reasons for the determination.\n>\n> **Where notification has not been made within that period, the determination has no force or effect**, and the alien is not excludable under clause (i) on any basis described in clause (iii). **The duty to notify is ministerial.**\""},{"anchor":"sec-201","heading":"\u00a7 201. The receiving sovereign must consent","part":"TITLE II \u2014 REMOVAL TO A THIRD COUNTRY","body":"> **(a) Consent required.** An alien may not be removed to a country of which the alien is not a national unless **that country has consented in writing to receive the alien**, the consent identifies the alien, and the consent is **executed by an official of that country with authority to bind it.**\n>\n> **(b) Consent to receive is not consent to detain.** Where the receiving country will detain, imprison, or restrict the liberty of the alien, **the written consent shall state the legal basis, the expected duration, and the conditions**, and shall be furnished to the alien and to the alien's counsel before removal.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE II \u2014 REMOVAL TO A THIRD COUNTRY","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1231.*"}]},{"anchor":"sec-202","heading":"\u00a7 202. Assurances, itemized","part":"TITLE II \u2014 REMOVAL TO A THIRD COUNTRY","body":"> **(c)** Where removal is to a country other than the country of nationality, the Secretary shall obtain and place in the record **written assurances** addressing, item by item: whether the alien will be detained; the conditions of any detention; whether the alien will have access to counsel; whether the alien may leave; and **whether the receiving country will permit return to the United States** if a court so orders.\n>\n> **(d)** An assurance that is general, oral, or conditional does not satisfy subsection (c), and **removal effected without assurances satisfying subsection (c) is unlawful.**"},{"anchor":"sec-203","heading":"\u00a7 203. Facilitation of return","part":"TITLE II \u2014 REMOVAL TO A THIRD COUNTRY","body":"> **(e)** Where a court determines that a removal was effected in violation of law, the Secretary **shall take all steps within the Secretary's authority to facilitate the alien's return to the United States**, including making and pursuing a formal request to the receiving government, and shall report to the court every **30 days** on the steps taken.\n>\n> **(f) Impossibility pleaded with specificity.** Where the Secretary asserts that return cannot be effected, the assertion shall state **what was requested, of whom, when, and the response received.** **Evidence that the United States has previously effected the return of any person from the same country is admissible against the assertion**, and a prior return from that country is evidence that return is available."},{"anchor":"sec-301","heading":"\u00a7 301. Capacity to comply is a condition of detaining","part":"TITLE III \u2014 DETENTION","body":"> **(a-1)** An alien may be detained under this section only where the detaining agency has **capacity to comply with every obligation the law imposes with respect to a detained person** \u2014 including access to counsel, provision of required medical care, and the conduct of hearings within required periods. **Where the agency lacks that capacity, the alien shall be released** on conditions.\n>\n> **(a-2)** The agency shall publish **monthly**, for each facility, its rated capacity, its population, and the number of detained persons for whom a required hearing has not been held within the required period. **The duty is ministerial and enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE III \u2014 DETENTION","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1226, and applies to detention under sections 1225(b) and 1231(a) of that title as \u00a7\u00a7 303 and 304 provide.*"}]},{"anchor":"sec-302","heading":"\u00a7 302. Detainers are requests","part":"TITLE III \u2014 DETENTION","body":"> **(a-3)** A detainer issued under this chapter is a **request**. It imposes no obligation on any State, local, or Tribal government or officer, and **no State, local, or Tribal government or officer incurs any liability, penalty, or loss of funds for declining to comply with it.** A detainer is not a basis on which any person may be held after the time the person would otherwise be released."},{"anchor":"sec-303","heading":"\u00a7 303. Periodic review of continued detention","part":"TITLE III \u2014 DETENTION","body":"**(a) The hearing.** A person detained under this chapter for the period set in the schedule to this Act **shall be given a hearing before the United States Immigration Court**, and a further hearing at each interval thereafter set in the schedule, for so long as the detention continues. **The hearing is provided without request**, and a person may also request one at any time on a showing of changed circumstances. **Where a hearing required by this subsection has not been held within 15 days after it is due, the person shall be released on conditions the Court sets on the record**, and the Government may thereafter seek continued detention at a hearing under this Section. **The duty to schedule is ministerial**, and the Court shall publish annually the number of hearings due, held, and held late.\n\n**(b) Burden.** At a hearing under subsection (a) **the Government bears the burden of establishing, by clear and convincing evidence, that no condition or combination of conditions of release will reasonably assure the person's appearance or the safety of any other person.** Detention that is not so justified shall end.\n\n**(c) Alternatives shall be considered.** The Court shall consider conditions of release \u2014 including supervision, reporting, electronic monitoring, bond, and community-based programs \u2014 **and shall order the least restrictive conditions that satisfy subsection (b).** A determination that detention is justified shall state why no condition would suffice.\n\n**(d) Notwithstanding mandatory-detention provisions.** This Section applies **notwithstanding section 1226(c) of this title and section 1225(b) of this title**, and without regard to the ground on which the person was detained. **This Section does not disturb the initial custody determination those provisions require**; it governs only whether detention may continue past the period in subsection (a).\n\n**(e) On the record, with counsel and interpretation.** A hearing under this Section is a proceeding of the Court. **Title V applies to it**, and Title VI applies to it, and the Court shall make a record.\n\n**(f) The record of decision.** A determination that detention shall continue shall state **the facts particular to that person** on which it rests, the conditions considered, and why each was insufficient. **A determination reciting the ground of detention, the person's immigration status, or the pendency of proceedings, without facts particular to that person, does not satisfy this subsection.**\n\n**(g) Cumulative time.** The periods in subsection (a) run from the beginning of detention under this chapter and are **not restarted** by a transfer, a change in the legal basis of detention, a new charging document, or the entry of a final order of removal."},{"anchor":"sec-304","heading":"\u00a7 304. Detention after a final order","part":"TITLE III \u2014 DETENTION","body":"**(a)** Where a person is detained after a final order of removal and removal has not been effected within the period set in the schedule to this Act, **\u00a7 303 applies**, and the Government bears the burden under \u00a7 303(b) together with the burden of establishing that removal remains **significantly likely in the reasonably foreseeable future.**\n\n**(b) Neither burden is satisfied by the other.** A showing that removal remains likely does not establish that detention is necessary, and a showing that the person is a flight risk does not establish that removal is foreseeable."},{"anchor":"sec-304a","heading":"\u00a7 304A. Alternatives to detention","part":"TITLE III \u2014 DETENTION","body":"**(a) Availability.** The Secretary shall maintain programs of supervision and support sufficient to permit release on conditions under \u00a7 303(c), including reporting, case management, legal-orientation services, and electronic monitoring where less restrictive conditions would not suffice. **The unavailability of a program the Secretary was required to maintain under this subsection is not a ground for finding that no condition of release would satisfy \u00a7 303(b)**.\n\n**(b) Least restrictive.** Electronic monitoring shall not be imposed where a less restrictive condition would reasonably assure appearance, and **the Court shall state on the record why a less restrictive condition would not.**\n\n**(c) Compliance published, in full.** The Secretary shall publish annually, by program and by location \u2014 the number of persons released on each condition; **the rate of appearance at scheduled proceedings**; the rate of compliance with reporting and other conditions; **the number who failed to appear and were not located**; and **the rate of compliance with a final order of removal** among persons released on each condition.\n\n**(c-1) Comparison to detention.** The Secretary shall publish the same measures for persons who were **detained** through the conclusion of proceedings.\n\n**(d) Failure to appear.** Nothing in this Title limits the consequence of a failure to appear where notice was actually received as Title IX requires."},{"anchor":"sec-305","heading":"\u00a7 305. Construction","part":"TITLE III \u2014 DETENTION","body":"**(a) Duration is reached through process.** This Title states **no maximum period of detention.** It requires that continued detention be justified to a court, on a record, at intervals \u2014 and that where it cannot be justified, it ends.\n\n**(b) Nothing compelled.** Nothing in this Title requires the release of a person as to whom the Government carries its burden, requires any person to be detained, or bears on removability or on any application for relief.\n\n**(b-1) Danger is a sufficient ground, and the Act does not limit proving it.** \u00a7 303(b) permits continued detention on clear and convincing evidence that **no condition of release will reasonably assure the safety of any other person.** Nothing in this Act limits the evidence the Government may offer to make that showing, and **a record of serious violent conduct is evidence a court may find sufficient.** This Act creates no presumption of release, no ceiling on detention where the showing is made, and no restriction on the Government's ability to make it. **The requirement is that the showing be made to a court on a record \u2014 not that it be difficult to make where the facts support it.**\n\n**(c) A statutory right.** The right created by this Title is statutory. **Its existence and content do not depend on the construction any court has given to any constitutional provision**, and nothing here states or implies what the Constitution requires as to the duration of detention.\n\n**(d) Severability.** \u00a7 303 and \u00a7 304 are severable from one another and from \u00a7\u00a7 301 and 302. **If \u00a7 303(d) is held invalid as to persons detained under section 1226(c), \u00a7 303 remains in force as to every other detained person.**"},{"anchor":"sec-401","heading":"\u00a7 401. Venue fixed at the point of the government's conduct","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 VENUE, JURISDICTION, AND THE RECORD","body":"> **(a)** For a petition for a writ of habeas corpus by a person in immigration custody, venue lies in the district in which the person was held **at the time of the conduct challenged**, or in which the decision to take that action was made, **at the petitioner's election.** **A transfer of the person after that time does not divest venue**, and where a person has been transferred without notice to counsel, venue lies in the district from which the person was transferred.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IV \u2014 VENUE, JURISDICTION, AND THE RECORD","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-402","heading":"\u00a7 402. Transfer does not defeat jurisdiction","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 VENUE, JURISDICTION, AND THE RECORD","body":"> **(b)** Once a petition has been filed, **the removal or transfer of the petitioner, within or outside the United States, does not divest the court of jurisdiction** and does not moot the petition. Where the petitioner has been removed, the court retains jurisdiction to order the relief provided in \u00a7 203."},{"anchor":"sec-403","heading":"\u00a7 403. Preservation","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 VENUE, JURISDICTION, AND THE RECORD","body":"> **(c)** Where a person is removed, detained, or transferred, the agency shall preserve for **10 years** the order or determination authorizing the action, any assurance obtained under \u00a7 202, any consent obtained under \u00a7 201, and the record of any communication with a foreign government concerning the person. **Records shall be furnished to the person and to the person's counsel on request.**"},{"anchor":"sec-501","heading":"\u00a7 501. Counsel appointed and paid by the United States","part":"TITLE V \u2014 THE RIGHT TO COUNSEL","body":"**(a) Right to counsel.** *Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1362.*\n\n> **\u00a7 1362. Right to counsel**\n>\n> In any removal proceedings before an immigration judge and in any appeal proceedings before the Attorney General**, the Board of Immigration Appeals, the Appellate Division of the United States Immigration Court, any other body or officer exercising any function of an immigration judge, of that Board, or of that Division, other than an officer who, without an immigration judge, completes a removal order, a withdrawal of an application for admission, or permission to depart, or any other body or officer reviewing a decision of any body or officer named or described in this sentence by certification, referral, appeal, or otherwise, other than a court established under article III of the Constitution,** from any such removal proceedings, the person concerned shall have the privilege of being represented ~~(at no expense to the Government)~~ by such counsel, authorized to practice in such proceedings, as ~~he~~ **the person concerned** shall choose.\n>\n> **In the case of a person described in section 503 of the Day in Court Act who is financially unable to obtain counsel, counsel shall be appointed to represent the person in those proceedings, in any custody or bond proceeding related to them, in a petition for a writ of habeas corpus under section 1252(e)(2) of this title, in a petition for review under section 1252 of this title raising a claim described in section 1252(a)(2)(E) of this title, and in an action brought before a final order is entered to enforce a right, duty, or condition established by that Act, as section 1252(b)(9) of this title permits. Counsel so appointed shall be compensated by the United States, from funds appropriated for that purpose, at rates and under procedures prescribed under section 506 of that Act. The United States shall bear the cost of counsel appointed under this paragraph, and no part of that cost shall be charged to, recovered from, or made a condition of relief for the person represented. An award of fees and expenses under section 2412 of title 28 for representation by counsel appointed under this paragraph shall be paid to the appropriation or organization from which that counsel was compensated, and is not included in the gross income of the person represented.**\n>\n> **For a financially eligible person not described in section 503 of the Day in Court Act, a petition for a writ of habeas corpus under section 1252(e)(2) of this title, a petition for review under section 1252 of this title raising a claim described in section 1252(a)(2)(E) of this title, or an action brought before a final order is entered to enforce a right, duty, or condition established by that Act, as section 1252(b)(9) of this title permits, shall be treated as a proceeding seeking relief under section 2241 of title 28 for purposes of section 3006A(a)(2)(B) of title 18, and for a petition for review the court of appeals in which it is filed is the court for purposes of section 3006A of title 18. Appointment and compensation under that provision remain subject to section 3006A of title 18, including the determination that the interests of justice require representation, and shall be paid from funds available under that section.**\n\n**(b) Naming does not confer authority.** Naming a body or officer in the amendment made by this section confers no authority and recognizes none, and does not limit \u00a7 705(c) or \u00a7 706(b), where those sections are in force, or any other provision of law limiting review of a decision of the United States Immigration Court or an immigration judge.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE V \u2014 THE RIGHT TO COUNSEL","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-502","heading":"\u00a7 502. Conforming amendment","part":"TITLE V \u2014 THE RIGHT TO COUNSEL","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1229a(b)(4)(A).*\n\n> \"(A) the alien shall have the privilege of being represented, ~~at no expense to the Government,~~ by counsel of the alien's choosing who is authorized to practice in such proceedings, **and, where the alien is described in section 503 of the Day in Court Act and is financially unable to obtain counsel, shall have counsel appointed and compensated as section 1362 of this title provides**\""},{"anchor":"sec-503","heading":"\u00a7 503. Persons entitled to appointed counsel","part":"TITLE V \u2014 THE RIGHT TO COUNSEL","body":"**(a) The general rule.** A person is entitled to appointed counsel under section 1362 of title 8 if the person held authorization to be present in the United States and **that authorization did not lapse according to its own stated terms.**\n\n**(b) Authorization ended by the government is not lapse.** For purposes of subsection (a), authorization has **not** lapsed according to its own terms where it was revoked, rescinded, terminated, or withdrawn by an officer of the United States, or where a status was not extended, renewed, or redesignated by a **decision of an officer of the United States** declining to do so. **Expiry of a status by operation of a statute, or because a statutory authority itself lapsed, is lapse according to its own terms** and is not within this subsection.\n\n**(c) Authorization.** Authorization to be present includes admission in any status, adjustment of status, lawful permanent residence, parole, temporary protected status, deferred action, deferred enforced departure, a grant of asylum or withholding, and any employment authorization issued by the United States.\n\n**(d) Minors.** A person who was under 18 years of age when proceedings commenced is entitled to appointed counsel **without regard to subsection (a)**.\n\n**(e) Competency.** A person whom the immigration judge finds unable to understand the nature of the proceedings or to assist in presenting the case is entitled to appointed counsel **without regard to subsection (a)**. Where the judge has reason to believe the person may be unable to do so, the judge shall **continue the proceeding and appoint counsel for the limited purpose of addressing the question**, and the proceeding shall not go forward on the merits until it is resolved. **The appointment is what resolves the question; the question does not have to be resolved to obtain the appointment.**\n\n**(f) No exception for a criminal conviction.** **Entitlement under this section does not depend on whether the person has been convicted of any offense.**\n\n**(g) Financial eligibility.** A person is financially unable to obtain counsel who so states under penalty of perjury on a form prescribed under section 506. **No further showing is required to obtain an appointment**, and an appointment shall not be delayed pending inquiry. An appointment may be terminated prospectively on a finding, stated on the record, that the statement was materially false."},{"anchor":"sec-504","heading":"\u00a7 504. Waiver","part":"TITLE V \u2014 THE RIGHT TO COUNSEL","body":"**(a) Requirements.** A person entitled to appointed counsel may waive it only in a waiver made **on the record**, in a language the person understands, after the immigration judge has stated that counsel is available at no cost to the person and has confirmed that the person understands it.\n\n**(b) Not at the first hearing.** A waiver shall not be accepted at the first hearing at which the person appears.\n\n**(c) Revocable.** A waiver may be withdrawn at any stage, and counsel shall then be appointed for the remainder of the proceedings.\n\n**(d) Silence is not waiver.** Failure to request counsel, failure to appear, and failure to complete a form are not a waiver."},{"anchor":"sec-505","heading":"\u00a7 505. Condition on the authority to order removal","part":"TITLE V \u2014 THE RIGHT TO COUNSEL","body":"**(a) The condition.** **An order of removal shall not be entered against a person entitled to appointed counsel under section 503 unless the person was represented by counsel at the proceeding or waived counsel under section 504.** This section conditions the authority to enter the order; it creates no cause of action and states no rule of decision on any question of removability or relief.\n\n**(b) An order entered in violation of subsection (a).** Such an order is of no effect: it may not be executed or reinstated, supports no ground of inadmissibility or ineligibility that depends on it, and bars no relief. Section 1231(a)(5) of title 8 does not bar a motion under this subsection and does not permit reinstatement of the order. The proceeding is reopened on motion of the person. **No numerical limit on motions to reopen, and no requirement of exhaustion, applies to a motion under this subsection.** A motion shall be filed within the period set in the schedule to this Act after the order, or after the date this Title takes effect, whichever is later.\n\n**(b-1) Prospective only as to the standing stock.** This section applies to an order entered **on or after the date this Title takes effect.** An order entered before that date is not subject to subsection (b), and this Title creates no ground to reopen it.\n\n**(c) Departure does not moot it.** A person removed under an order described in subsection (b) may file the motion from outside the United States, and **the person's absence is not a ground to deny it**. Where the motion is granted, the Secretary shall facilitate the person's return as section 203 provides.\n\n**(d) Criminal use of an order of no effect.** An order of no effect under subsection (b) shall not be treated as an order of exclusion, deportation, or removal for purposes of section 1326 of title 8, and a removal, deportation, or departure under it shall not be treated as a removal, deportation, or departure for purposes of subsection (a) or (b) of that section. Nothing in this subsection limits the absence of effect of such an order for any other purpose. The defect rendering the order of no effect may be raised as a defense in that prosecution notwithstanding section 1326(d) of that title and any other provision of law limiting a challenge to the order in that prosecution, without satisfying any condition of section 1326(d) and without a prior grant of a motion to reopen. Where the defendant identifies the defect, the Government bears the burden of establishing, from the record of the proceeding, that the condition in subsection (a) was satisfied."},{"anchor":"sec-506","heading":"\u00a7 506. Administration outside the Department of Justice","part":"TITLE V \u2014 THE RIGHT TO COUNSEL","body":"**(a) Administering authority.** The **Director of the Administrative Office of the United States Courts** shall administer appointment and compensation of counsel under section 1362 of title 8, on the model of section 3006A of title 18, including panels of qualified counsel, rates of compensation, forms, and the prescribing of financial-eligibility procedures.\n\n**(b) No party controls opposing counsel.** **A party to a proceeding shall not select, compensate, evaluate, or terminate the counsel appointed for the opposing party**, and no officer or employee of the Department of Justice or of the Department of Homeland Security shall do any of those things under this Title.\n\n**(c) Organizations.** Appointment may be made of an attorney employed by a legal-services organization, a law-school clinic, or a non-profit organization qualified under the procedures prescribed under subsection (a). **Compensation is paid to the organization**, and receiving it does not make the organization an agent or contractor of either Department.\n\n**(d) Sequencing where funds are insufficient.** Where appropriations are insufficient to appoint counsel for every person entitled, **appointments shall be made first for persons in detention**, and the Director shall publish annually the number of persons entitled, the number for whom counsel was appointed, and the number for whom counsel was not appointed for want of funds."},{"anchor":"sec-507","heading":"\u00a7 507. Appropriation","part":"TITLE V \u2014 THE RIGHT TO COUNSEL","body":"**(a)** There are authorized to be appropriated such sums as are necessary to carry out this Title.\n\n**(b) Continuity.** The compensation of counsel already appointed is an activity authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations, and obligations incurred for it are authorized in advance of appropriations for purposes of sections 1341(a)(1)(B) and 1342 of title 31, United States Code. **A person shall not lose appointed counsel mid-proceeding because a fiscal year ended.**"},{"anchor":"sec-508","heading":"\u00a7 508. Construction","part":"TITLE V \u2014 THE RIGHT TO COUNSEL","body":"**(a) A statutory right.** The right created by this Title is statutory. **Its existence and content do not depend on the construction any court has given to any provision of the Constitution addressing the assistance of counsel**, and nothing in this Title states or implies that the Constitution requires or does not require appointed counsel in a removal proceeding.\n\n**(b) No entitlement beyond section 503.** This Title obliges the United States to appoint and compensate counsel only for a person described in section 503. For every other person, section 1362 of title 8 continues to provide the privilege of being represented by counsel of the person's choosing.\n\n**(c) Quality of representation.** Nothing in this Title creates a standard of performance for appointed counsel, a claim for ineffective assistance, or a ground for relief other than as section 505 provides.\n\n**(d) Severability.** Each section of this Title is severable. **If section 503(d) or (e) is held invalid, the entitlement under section 503(a) is unaffected**, and if section 506(a) is held invalid, the entitlement continues and the President shall designate an administering authority that is not the Department of Justice or the Department of Homeland Security.\n\n**(e) Onset.** This Title applies to proceedings commenced on or after the date set in the schedule to this Act, which shall be not earlier than one year after enactment. The Director shall establish the panels, rates, forms, and procedures required by section 506 before that date."},{"anchor":"sec-509","heading":"\u00a7 509. Preparers and filings","part":"TITLE V \u2014 THE RIGHT TO COUNSEL","body":"**(a) Identification.** Every application, motion, or filing prepared for a person by another shall **identify the preparer by name, address, and any registration or bar number**, and state whether a fee was charged and in what amount. **A filing that does not identify its preparer may be corrected without prejudice**, and no consequence attaches to the person on whose behalf it was filed.\n\n**(b) Knowingly false filings.** A person who prepares a filing **knowing it to contain a materially false statement**, or who charges a fee for a filing the person knows to be frivolous, is subject to a civil penalty in an amount set in the schedule to this Act, payable to the United States, and to the remedies in subsection (d).\n\n**(c) Holding out.** A person who is not an attorney or an accredited representative shall not hold out that person as authorized to represent anyone in a proceeding under this Act. **The use of a title suggesting legal qualification, including one whose meaning differs in another language from its meaning in the United States, is such a holding out.**\n\n**(d) The person harmed is made whole, not punished.** Where a filing violated subsection (b) or (c) \u2014\n\n1. **no adverse consequence attaches to the person on whose behalf it was filed** by reason of the filing, including for purposes of \u00a7 1103 or any bar arising from a frivolous or fraudulent application;\n2. the person may **reopen and refile** without regard to any time or numerical limit; and\n3. the preparer is liable to the person for **the fee charged and the damages caused.**\n\n**(f) Not a limit on assistance.** Nothing in this Section restricts assistance given without charge by a family member, a community organization, a law-school clinic, or an accredited representative, or the preparation of a filing by an attorney."},{"anchor":"sec-601","heading":"\u00a7 601. Interpretation in immigration proceedings","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INTERPRETATION AND TRANSLATION","body":"**(a) Proceedings under the Court Interpreters Act.**\n\n**(1) Proceedings covered.** *Amends 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 1827(j).*\n\n> \"The term 'judicial proceedings instituted by the United States' as used in this section refers to all proceedings, whether criminal or civil, including pretrial and grand jury proceedings (as well as proceedings upon a petition for a writ of habeas corpus initiated in the name of the United States by a relator) conducted in, or pursuant to the lawful authority and jurisdiction of a United States district court**, and to any proceeding, whether commenced by the United States or otherwise, before an immigration judge, the Board of Immigration Appeals, the Appellate Division of the United States Immigration Court, any other body or officer exercising any function of an immigration judge, of that Board, or of that Division, other than an officer who, without an immigration judge, completes a removal order, a withdrawal of an application for admission, or permission to depart, the Attorney General or any other body or officer reviewing a decision of any body or officer named or described in this sentence by certification, referral, appeal, or otherwise, or an asylum officer or any other officer conducting an interview on, or adjudicating, a claim for asylum, withholding of removal, or protection under the Convention Against Torture, or conducting a fear-screening interview, including a custody or bond proceeding and a credible-fear or reasonable-fear interview. The extension to immigration proceedings made by the preceding sentence does not include a proceeding before a court established under article III of the Constitution. In this subsection, a 'fear-screening interview' means an interview or process applying a credible-fear, reasonable-fear, or other screening standard to a claim of fear of persecution, torture, or return; it does not include inspection or processing at which an officer asks whether a person fears return without applying a screening standard to the claim**. The term 'United States district court' as used in this subsection includes any court which is created by an Act of Congress in a territory and is invested with any jurisdiction of a district court established by chapter 5 of this title.\"\n\n**(2) The officer who holds the duty.** *Amends 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 1827(i).*\n\n> \"The term 'presiding judicial officer' as used in this section refers to any judge of a United States district court, including a bankruptcy judge, a United States magistrate judge, and in the case of grand jury proceedings conducted under the auspices of the United States attorney, a United States attorney. **In a proceeding before any body or officer named or described in subsection (j), other than a court, the term also refers to the member or members of the body, or the officer, before whom the proceeding is conducted. In such a proceeding, the responsibilities this section assigns to the clerk of the court are those of the official responsible for that proceeding under section 607(a) of the Day in Court Act.**\"\n\n**(3) Requests for certification.** *Amends 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 1827(b)(1).*\n\n> \"(1) The Director shall prescribe, determine, and certify the qualifications of persons who may serve as certified interpreters, when the Director considers certification of interpreters to be merited, for the hearing impaired (whether or not also speech impaired) and persons who speak only or primarily a language other than the English language, in judicial proceedings instituted by the United States. The Director may certify interpreters for any language if the Director determines that there is a need for certified interpreters in that language. Upon the request of the Judicial Conference of the United States for certified interpreters in a language, the Director shall certify interpreters in that language. **Upon the request of the Chief Judge of the United States Immigration Court for certified interpreters in a language, the Director shall certify interpreters in that language. Upon the request of the Director of the Executive Office for Immigration Review for certified interpreters in a language, where the transfer under section 704(b) of the Day in Court Act has not taken effect or Title VII of that Act is not in force, the Director shall certify interpreters in that language.** Upon such a request from the judicial council of a circuit and the approval of the Judicial Conference, the Director shall certify interpreters for that circuit in the language requested. The judicial council of a circuit shall identify and evaluate the needs of the districts within a circuit. The Director shall certify interpreters based on the results of criterion-referenced performance examinations. The Director shall issue regulations to carry out this paragraph within 1 year after the date of the enactment of the Judicial Improvements and Access to Justice Act.\"\n\n**(4) The person's statement.** In a proceeding brought within section 1827(j) of title 28 by paragraph (1), the person's statement on the record that the person speaks only or primarily a language other than English **shall be treated as the determination under section 1827(d)(1) of that title, including the determination that the person's language inhibits the person's comprehension of the proceedings or communication with counsel or the presiding judicial officer, that interpretation is required**. No contrary assessment of the person's English displaces that statement. At the first hearing or interview in the proceeding held on or after the date this paragraph applies to it, the presiding judicial officer, within the meaning of section 1827(i) of that title, shall ask the person, through an interpreter or a written language-identification aid, what language the person understands, and shall record the inquiry and the person's answer. Where the record does not show that inquiry and the person's answer, interpretation shall be treated as required.\n\n**(b) Interpretation in officer proceedings.**\n\n**(1) The duty.** In an officer proceeding, the United States shall provide interpretation, by an interpreter qualified under section 1827 of title 28, in a language the person understands, of every oral exchange by which an order is entered, a withdrawal is accepted, or permission to depart is granted. This duty includes inspection, questioning, a sworn statement, service and any explanation of a notice, and every interview or taking of a statement in the officer proceeding.\n\n**(2) Interpretation unless declined.** Interpretation is required for every person unless the person states that no interpreter is needed. No determination of need by an officer is a prerequisite to the duty.\n\n**(3) The record.** The officer shall record the language the person states that the person understands, the identity of each interpreter, and the basis of each interpreter's qualification. A statement that no interpreter is needed shall be recorded in the person's own words, together with the language in which the statement was made. **A conclusion, a checked box, or an officer's summary does not satisfy this paragraph, and an unrecorded statement is not a statement for purposes of paragraph (2) or section 605.** A statement that no interpreter is needed is effective only if the officer first offered interpretation, through an interpreter or a written language-identification aid, in a language the person indicated, and the offer and the response are recorded.\n\n**(4) Qualification alone is borrowed.** Section 1827 of title 28 does not otherwise apply to an officer proceeding. Its presiding-judicial-officer requirements, certification-tier preference, interpretation-mode requirements, waiver procedure under subsection (f), and cost rules under subsection (g) do not apply to that proceeding.\n\n**(c) Definitions.** For purposes of this Title \u2014\n\n**(1) Officer proceeding.** An **officer proceeding** is the process by which an officer completes a removal order, a withdrawal of an application for admission, or permission to depart without an immigration judge, regardless of the authority invoked or the form, title, or characterization of the action. It includes the person's request for or agreement to the action, and every encounter, interview, statement taken, notice, and service forming part of the process. It excludes any part that is a proceeding brought within section 1827(j) of title 28 by subsection (a). In this Title, a removal order includes any order, direction, or determination by which a person is to be removed, returned, or expelled from the United States under any authority, including section 1182(f) of title 8 and section 265 of title 42, and the inclusion of each named authority is severable from the others.\n\n**(2) Included instances.** Officer proceedings include \u2014\n\n**(A)** entry of a removal order under section 1225(b)(1) of title 8;\n\n**(B)** entry of a removal order under section 1228(b) of that title;\n\n**(C)** reinstatement under section 1231(a)(5) of that title, meaning the reinstatement itself and not the prior order; and\n\n**(D)** permission granted by an officer, rather than an immigration judge, to depart under section 1229c of that title, including permission labeled voluntary return, and the person's request for or agreement to it.\n\n**(3) Officer.** An **officer** is any officer or employee of the United States, and any other person exercising an immigration officer's function under an agreement, delegation, or contract.\n\n**(4) Record of the officer proceeding.** The **record of the officer proceeding** comprises every writing the officer makes or relies on in the proceeding, including any sworn statement, notice, order, withdrawal, permission to depart, and entry required by subsection (b)(3), and any recording of the proceeding.\n\n**(5) Qualified interpreter outside the Court Interpreters Act.** When used outside a proceeding described in section 1827(j) of title 28, **an interpreter qualified under section 1827 of title 28** means a natural person who is certified under section 1827(b) of that title, who meets the published criteria the Director of the Administrative Office of the United States Courts applies to professionally qualified interpreters, or who has passed an assessment of interpretation ability in the language that the Director administers or approves. The recorded basis of qualification shall identify the certification, credential, or assessment relied on, by issuer and number. An officer's or employing agency's own assessment of an interpreter's ability, and a vendor's attestation, are not a basis of qualification. No determination by a presiding judicial officer is required; the basis of qualification shall be recorded under subsection (b)(3), or, for a reading under section 603, in the record required by section 603(b).\n\n**(6) Permission to depart.** **Permission to depart** means an officer's authorization or acceptance of a person's departure as a disposition of immigration inspection, processing, or proceedings, including voluntary departure or voluntary return, however labeled, and including an acceptance through any process or automated system operated by or for the Department of Homeland Security, which is an acceptance by an officer for purposes of this Title. It includes the person's request for or agreement to that disposition.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VI \u2014 INTERPRETATION AND TRANSLATION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-602","heading":"\u00a7 602. Written material","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INTERPRETATION AND TRANSLATION","body":"**(a) Documents presented for signature.** No document shall be presented to a person for signature in a proceeding described in section 1827(j) of title 28 or in an officer proceeding, or for use in such a proceeding, including a stipulation to removal and any waiver of a hearing, appeal, or relief, unless it has been **translated in writing into a language the person understands**, and the translation is furnished to the person and retained in the record.\n\n**(b) Documents that determine rights.** A notice to appear, a notice of hearing, a decision, an order, and any document stating a deadline or a consequence of failing to act shall be furnished **in writing in a language the person understands**, at the time it is furnished in English.\n\n**(c) No adverse consequence of a signature obtained otherwise.** A signature obtained in violation of subsection (a) **does not constitute agreement, waiver, concession, admission, withdrawal, or consent to anything adverse to the person, and no consequence adverse to the person shall attach to it.** The person may elect to be bound by the document. In the absence of that election, favorable terms remain binding on the presenting party, and adverse terms do not bind the person. An election to be bound is effective only if made after the document has been translated, or read as section 603 requires, in a language the person understands, after the person is told on the record that the person is not otherwise bound, and in a statement recorded in the person's own words. An election made at the time of the signing it would ratify is not an election. A signature obtained without a reading section 603 requires is obtained in violation of subsection (a).\n\n**(d) What \"a language the person understands\" means.** Throughout this Title, **a language the person understands** means the language the person states, on the record, that the person understands. **The determination is the person's, not the agency's**, and an agency's assessment of the person's English does not displace it. In an officer proceeding, \"on the record\" means in the record of the officer proceeding defined in section 601(c)(4).\n\n**(e) Periods run from the material furnished as required.** Notwithstanding section 1252(b)(1) of title 8 and any other provision of law, a period within which the person may or must act in response to a document described in subsection (b), including a period for seeking review, and a period that must elapse before an order may be executed, shall begin only when the document has been furnished in English and in writing in a language the person understands as subsection (b) requires and, where section 603 applies, has been read aloud as that section requires. **This subsection does not extend a period that runs in the person's favor**, including a period within which the Government must act or after which the person becomes entitled to release, relief, or another benefit."},{"anchor":"sec-603","heading":"\u00a7 603. Where the person cannot read","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INTERPRETATION AND TRANSLATION","body":"**(a) Reading aloud.** Where a person is unable to read a document presented for signature, or a document of a kind listed in section 602(b), in a proceeding brought within section 1827(j) of title 28 by section 601(a) or in an officer proceeding, the document shall be **read aloud in full, in a language the person understands, by an interpreter qualified under section 1827 of title 28**, before any signature is sought and before any deadline stated in it begins to run. This duty applies whether or not section 602 is yet applicable to the document. Where no written translation is yet required, the duty requires an oral translation in full of the English document.\n\n**(b) Recorded.** The fact that a document was read aloud, the language used, the identity of the interpreter, the basis of the interpreter's qualification, and the date shall be **recorded in the proceeding or in the record of the officer proceeding, as applicable**. A statement that a document was \"explained\" or \"summarized\" does not satisfy subsection (a).\n\n**(c) No inquiry into literacy is required.** A person may request that a document be read aloud without stating a reason and without any showing, **and the request shall be granted.** In an officer proceeding, before a document described in subsection (a) is presented to the person, the officer shall inform the person, in a language the person understands, that the person may have the document read aloud, and shall record the person's answer in the record of the officer proceeding."},{"anchor":"sec-604","heading":"\u00a7 604. The oath applies to everyone","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INTERPRETATION AND TRANSLATION","body":"*Amends 8 C.F.R. \u00a7 1003.22, and supersedes it to the extent of any conflict.*\n\n> \"Any person acting as an interpreter in a hearing shall swear or affirm to interpret and translate accurately~~, unless the interpreter is an employee of the United States Government, in which event no such oath or affirmation shall be required~~. **This requirement applies without regard to whether the interpreter is an employee of the United States Government.**\"\n\n*Amends 8 C.F.R. \u00a7 1240.5, and supersedes it to the extent of any conflict.*\n\n> \"Any person acting as an interpreter in a hearing before an immigration judge under this part shall be sworn to interpret and translate accurately~~, unless the interpreter is an employee of the United States Government, in which event no such oath shall be required~~. **This requirement applies without regard to whether the interpreter is an employee of the United States Government.**\"\n\n**No rule, form, or practice, whenever adopted, may exempt an interpreter from the oath or affirmation because the interpreter is an employee of the United States Government, and any such rule, form, or practice is without effect to the extent of the exemption. Every person acting as an interpreter in a proceeding brought within section 1827(j) of title 28 by section 601(a), or in an officer proceeding, shall swear or affirm to interpret and translate accurately, whether or not an employee of the United States, and the oath or affirmation shall be recorded.**"},{"anchor":"sec-605","heading":"\u00a7 605. Condition on the authority","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INTERPRETATION AND TRANSLATION","body":"**(a) The conditions.**\n\n**(1) Proceedings under the Court Interpreters Act.** An order of removal shall not be entered against a person in a proceeding in which interpretation is required by section 1827 of title 28, as extended by section 601(a), if the proceeding was conducted without interpretation **by an interpreter qualified under section 1827 of title 28, in a language the person understands**, unless the person waived interpretation in a statement recorded in the person's own words that satisfied section 1827(f)(1) of title 28. A deviation from a certification-tier preference or interpretation-mode requirement alone does not render the order of no effect under subsection (b). Those requirements remain binding and enforceable under sections 607 and 608. An order entered or reinstated following a proceeding brought within section 1827(j) of title 28 by section 601(a) is entered in that proceeding for purposes of this paragraph and subsection (b)(1), and a motion under subsection (b)(2) may raise a failure in that proceeding.\n\n**(2) Officer proceedings.** No removal order may be entered or reinstated, no withdrawal of an application for admission may be accepted, and no permission to depart or request for or agreement to such permission may be granted or accepted, where the officer proceeding was conducted without the interpretation section 601(b) requires, unless the person made a statement that no interpreter was needed in the recorded form section 601(b)(3) requires. Where the officer proceeding permits a written response or statement, the officer shall accept a response or statement in a language the person understands and translate it into English at the expense of the United States, notwithstanding any rule requiring English. Conducting the proceeding without accepting and translating that response or statement is conducting it without the interpretation required by this paragraph. The original and the translation shall be placed in the record of the officer proceeding, and the translation shall be furnished to the person. A reading section 603 requires that was not given in an officer proceeding is interpretation section 601(b) requires that was not provided. A statement taken from a person in an encounter or interview under section 1225, 1228, 1229c, or 1231(a)(5) of title 8 may not be offered against the person, or used to impeach the person, in any proceeding under title 8 or this Act unless the record shows that the statement was taken through an interpreter qualified under section 1827 of title 28, in a language the person understands, or that the person declined interpretation in the form section 601(b)(3) requires.\n\n**This section conditions the authority to enter or reinstate the order, accept the withdrawal, or grant or accept permission to depart; it states no rule of decision on removability or relief.**\n\n**(b) Consequences.**\n\n**(1) Proceedings under the Court Interpreters Act.** An order entered in violation of subsection (a)(1) is of no effect: it may not be executed or reinstated, supports no ground of inadmissibility or ineligibility that depends on it, and bars no relief. Section 1231(a)(5) of title 8 does not bar a motion under this paragraph and does not permit reinstatement of the order. The proceeding is reopened on motion of the person, subject only to the period in paragraph (3), and a motion concerning an order entered under section 1225(b)(1) of title 8 shall be decided as paragraph (2) provides for a motion under that paragraph, notwithstanding the provisions named there. A person removed under such an order may file the motion from outside the United States, and **the person's absence is not a ground to deny it**. Where the motion is granted, the Secretary shall facilitate the person's return as section 203 provides.\n\n**(2) Officer proceedings.** A removal order entered or reinstated in violation of subsection (a)(2) is **of no effect**: it may not be executed or reinstated, supports no ground of inadmissibility or ineligibility that depends on it, and bars no relief. Where the violation concerns a reinstatement, this consequence reaches only the reinstatement and not the prior order. A withdrawal, permission to depart, request for or agreement to either, or waiver given to obtain either, in violation of subsection (a)(2) carries **no consequence adverse to the person**, including any consequence of failure to depart, any inadmissibility under section 1182(a)(9) of title 8 arising from a departure under the withdrawal or permission, or waiver of a proceeding under section 1229a of title 8, unless the person elects to be bound in the form section 602(c) requires. Favorable terms remain binding on the United States in the absence of that election. Any amount collected on account of a consequence this paragraph or section 602(c) bars, including a civil penalty under section 1229c(d) of title 8, a forfeited voluntary departure bond, or a reduction of an overpayment under section 6402(d) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, shall be refunded by the agency to which it was paid.\n\nNotwithstanding sections 1225(b)(1)(A)(i), (B)(iii)(I), and (C), 1231(a)(5), and 1252(a)(2)(A), (e)(1), and (g) of title 8, and any other provision of law limiting administrative appeal, review, or reopening, solely for purposes of a motion under this paragraph, the United States Immigration Court shall determine the violation and give effect to these consequences on the person's motion, from the record of the officer proceeding. Before the transfer under section 704(b) takes effect, or where Title VII is not in force, an immigration judge however constituted shall decide the motion. The officer may begin a new proceeding in compliance with this Title. With every removal order, reinstatement, withdrawal, or permission to depart in an officer proceeding, the officer shall furnish the person, in a language the person understands, written notice of the motion under this paragraph and a copy of the record of the officer proceeding. A record of the officer proceeding that does not contain the entries section 601(b)(3) requires establishes the violation. Within 30 days after service of a motion under this paragraph, the Secretary shall file the complete record of the officer proceeding with the Court or immigration judge deciding the motion and serve it on the person, and where the Secretary does not, the violation is established. The person may submit a sworn statement of what occurred in the officer proceeding. **A motion may be filed from outside the United States, and the person's absence is not a ground to deny it. Where a motion concerning a removal order, reinstatement, withdrawal, or permission to depart is granted, the Secretary shall facilitate the person's return as section 203 provides.**\n\nThis paragraph confers no jurisdiction on a court established under article III of the Constitution to review an individual order under section 1225(b)(1) of title 8 on a ground not authorized by section 1252(e) of that title. Review of orders under section 1228(b) of title 8 and reinstatements under section 1231(a)(5) of that title remains available on petition for review under section 1252 of title 8.\n\n**(3) Period and procedure.** A motion under paragraph (1) or (2) shall be filed within the filing period provided by section 505(b), as reserved in item 7 of the schedule to this Act, after the order, reinstatement, withdrawal, or permission to depart, or after the date set in item 11 of that schedule, whichever is later. The same period reserved in item 7 applies if Title V is not in force. No numerical limit on motions to reopen and no requirement of exhaustion applies. Section 606(f), and not section 505(b-1), governs application in time.\n\n**(c) Interpretation is not counsel.** Nothing in this Title relieves the United States of any obligation under Title V, and the presence of an interpreter is not a substitute for counsel or a ground to find a waiver of it.\n\n**(d) Appeals and reviews on the papers.** In a proceeding brought within section 1827(j) of title 28 by section 601(a), an appeal or review **on the papers without a hearing** means an appeal or review decided from written submissions and the existing record, without an oral hearing. An **opposing party** is any party other than the person whose removal, custody, or claim for relief is at issue, including the United States. A **filing** is any submission to the reviewing body or officer, including a brief, motion, response, attachment, or evidence.\n\nWhere interpretation was required before the immigration judge, or the person states on the record of the appeal or review that the person speaks only or primarily a language other than English \u2014\n\n**(1)** where the person has no counsel of record, the reviewing body or officer shall furnish every opposing-party filing to the person in writing in a language the person understands, at the time it is furnished in English, and any period for the person's response runs from that furnishing;\n\n**(2)** a filing the person makes without counsel in a language the person understands shall be accepted as filed and translated into English by the reviewing body or officer at the expense of the United States, notwithstanding any rule requiring English, and the original and the translation shall be placed in the record and the translation furnished to the person; and\n\n**(3)** any hearing held shall be interpreted as subsection (a)(1) requires.\n\nA person's statement on the record before the immigration judge that no interpreter was needed carries forward only if the statement itself was recorded, rather than a conclusion or summary about it, and it satisfied the waiver requirements of section 1827(f)(1) of title 28 where those requirements applied. **The person may withdraw the statement on request, without stating a reason and without any showing, and the request shall be granted.** Upon withdrawal, the statement no longer excuses interpretation on the appeal or review, and paragraphs (1) through (3) apply where applicable. An order of removal entered on the papers without compliance with paragraph (1), (2), or (3), where applicable, is entered in a proceeding conducted without interpretation under subsection (a)(1).\n\n**(e) Criminal use of an order of no effect.** An order of no effect under subsection (b) of this section shall not be treated as an order of exclusion, deportation, or removal for purposes of section 1326 of title 8, and a removal, deportation, or departure under it shall not be treated as a removal, deportation, or departure for purposes of subsection (a) or (b) of that section. Nothing in this subsection limits the absence of effect of such an order for any other purpose. **The defect rendering the order of no effect may be raised as a defense in that prosecution notwithstanding section 1225(b)(1)(D) and section 1326(d) of that title and any other provision of law limiting a challenge to the order in that prosecution**, without satisfying any condition of section 1326(d) and without a prior grant of a motion to reopen or of a motion under subsection (b). Where the defendant identifies the defect, the Government bears the burden of establishing, from the record of the proceeding or the record of the officer proceeding, that the condition in subsection (a) was satisfied."},{"anchor":"sec-606","heading":"\u00a7 606. Construction","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INTERPRETATION AND TRANSLATION","body":"**(a) Every person.** This Title applies to **every person in a proceeding described in section 1827(j) of title 28, and every person in an officer proceeding**, without regard to authorization, status, manner of entry, entitlement to counsel, age, competency, criminal history, or financial means.\n\n**(b) Cost.** The cost of interpretation and translation under this Title is borne by the United States and **shall not be charged to, recovered from, or made a condition of relief for** the person.\n\n**No rule, form, or practice, whenever adopted, may require the person to provide, procure, or pay for an interpreter or translator, or attach an adverse consequence to the person's failure to do so, and any such rule, form, or practice, including section 208.9(g)(1) of title 8, Code of Federal Regulations, is without effect to the extent of the conflict.**\n\n**(c) Continuity.** Interpretation and translation in a pending proceeding are activities authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations. Obligations for those activities are authorized in advance of appropriations for purposes of sections 1341(a)(1)(B) and 1342 of title 31, United States Code. A person shall not lose interpretation or translation in a pending proceeding because a fiscal year ended.\n\n**(d) Severability.** Each section of this Title is severable. **If section 602 is held invalid in whole or part, sections 601 and 603 are unaffected, and the extension made by section 601(a) stands independently of every other provision of this Title.** Each instance in section 601(c)(2) is severable from the others and from section 601(a), and neither its omission nor its invalidity limits the functional definition in section 601(c)(1). The Chief Judge's and the Executive Office for Immigration Review Director's authority to request certification under section 601(a)(3) are severable from each other. Section 601(b) and the officer-proceeding applications of sections 602, 603, and 605 are severable from the applications to proceedings brought within section 1827(j) of title 28 by section 601(a), in both directions. Section 605(d), the recording and publication duties in section 607, its audit duty and entry condition, and each cause of action in section 608 are severable from each other and from the remainder of this Title. Invalidity of the membership-organization action does not affect a person's own action under section 608(c), and invalidity of either action does not affect the information action under section 608(b). Congress would have enacted each independently; their aggregation supplies no basis for invalidating a provision otherwise capable of operating.\n\n**(e) Appropriation.** There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary for **interpreter compensation, translator compensation, and the costs of translation required by this Title**, to the United States Immigration Court for its proceedings; to the Executive Office for Immigration Review for its proceedings where the transfer under section 704(b) has not taken effect or Title VII is not in force; and to the Department of Homeland Security for asylum-officer proceedings, proceedings before other officers performing the asylum or fear-screening functions described in section 601(a), and officer proceedings. Amounts are appropriated permanently and indefinitely, are available without further appropriation and without fiscal-year limitation, and do not lapse. Each cost shall be paid once, from the account of the forum conducting the proceeding.\n\nSection 1827(g)(2) of title 28 does not apply to proceedings brought within section 1827(j) of that title by section 601(a). The provisions of section 1827 charging interpreter costs to appropriations of the Federal judiciary or to a party, including subsection (g)(1), (3), and (4), do not govern costs payable under this subsection. Section 1827(h) continues to govern approval of compensation and expenses. **Certification and the development and administration of certification examinations remain funded under section 1827(g) and subject to section 1827(g)(2); no amount under this subsection is available for those costs.**\n\nAmounts under this subsection are in addition to, and shall not be derived from, amounts otherwise available for enforcement of the immigration laws. No reduction in enforcement funding is authorized by this subsection. The Inspector General of the Department of Homeland Security shall annually audit that Department's obligations under this subsection and publish the findings.\n\n**(f) Application in time.**\n\n**(1)** Sections 601, 603, and 604, the definition in section 602(d), and subsections (a) through (e) and (g) through (j) of this section take effect on enactment. They apply to steps taken on or after enactment in every covered proceeding, including a proceeding pending on enactment. The definition in section 602(d) applies wherever its defined term is used; its application does not advance the written-translation duties in section 602.\n\n**(2)** Sections 602, 605, 607, and 608 apply on and after the date set in item 11 of the schedule to this Act. Sections 602 and 605 apply to each document furnished or presented for signature and to each hearing, interview, or other proceeding step held or taken on or after that date, **in any proceeding whenever begun**. The recording, publication, audit, and entry requirements of section 607 and the actions under section 608 apply to conduct on or after that date, including in pending proceedings.\n\n**(3)** An order entered or reinstated, a withdrawal accepted, or permission to depart granted on or after that date is subject to section 605(b) **only for a failure at a step held or taken on or after that date**, and never for a failure occurring before it. No order entered before that date is rendered of no effect by section 605. No definition of when a proceeding commenced limits paragraphs (1) or (2).\n\n**(g) Naming does not confer authority.** Naming a body or officer in section 1827(j) of title 28 confers no authority and recognizes none. It does not limit section 705(c), section 706(b), where those sections are in force, or any other provision of law limiting review of a decision of the United States Immigration Court or an immigration judge.\n\n**(h) Rights under this Act.** A right or duty under section 1827 of title 28 in a proceeding brought within subsection (j) of that section by section 601(a), including as extended by section 601(a)(2) and (4), **shall be treated as established by the Day in Court Act** for purposes of section 1252(a)(2)(E) and (b)(9) of title 8, as amended by this Act, and Title X.\n\n**(i) Return where Title II is not in force.** If this Title is enacted without Title II, or Title II is not in force, the duty to facilitate return in section 605(b) means that the Secretary shall take all steps within the Secretary's authority to facilitate the person's return, including making and pursuing a formal request to the receiving government, and shall report to the court every 30 days on the steps taken. An assertion that return cannot be effected shall state what was requested, of whom, when, and the response received. Evidence of a prior return from the same country is admissible against that assertion and is evidence that return is available.\n\n**(j) Review where Title X is not in force.** If this Title is enacted without Title X, or Title X is not in force, section 1252(a)(2)(B) and (C) of title 8 do not preclude review on a petition for review of a claim that a right, duty, or condition under this Title was not satisfied, including a material question of fact. Notwithstanding section 1252(b)(4)(A) and (B) of that title, the reviewing court may determine that question on the administrative record and on further evidence concerning what the record does or does not contain, and shall determine de novo whether the record establishes a predicate this Title requires. This subsection changes no standard governing removability or discretionary relief and authorizes no Article III review of an individual order under section 1225(b)(1) of title 8 beyond section 1252(e) of that title."},{"anchor":"sec-607","heading":"\u00a7 607. Records, publication, and audit of interpretation","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INTERPRETATION AND TRANSLATION","body":"**(a) Responsible officials and records.** The Chief Judge of the United States Immigration Court, the Director of the Executive Office for Immigration Review before the transfer under section 704(b) takes effect or where Title VII is not in force, and the Secretary of Homeland Security for proceedings conducted by that Department, including proceedings conducted by any officer described in section 601(c)(3), shall carry out this section for their respective proceedings. A body or officer succeeding to any of those functions holds the same duties. **Every order in a proceeding covered by this Title shall record, for every hearing or oral interview to which the order relates, each interpreter's identity, language, certification tier, and interpretation mode**, or that interpretation was not required and the recorded basis. The same information shall be recorded when each hearing or oral interview is held, whether or not an order is entered.\n\nFor purposes of this section, **certification tier** means certification under section 1827(b) of title 28, otherwise-qualified status under that section or section 601(c)(5), or absence of either qualification. **Interpretation mode** means simultaneous or consecutive interpretation, separately identifying interpretation in person, by video, or by telephone. The record shall include each finding of certified-interpreter unavailability and any determination authorizing departure from the mode section 1827(k) of title 28 otherwise requires. In an officer proceeding, recording a tier or mode does not impose a tier preference or mode requirement.\n\n**(b) Annual publication.** Each official named in subsection (a) shall publish annually for the preceding calendar year, in a form accessible to the public and in a machine-readable form that includes de-identified hearing-level records, **separately for each court and language**, the number of hearings and oral interviews held, the number at which interpretation was required, the number interpreted by each certification tier and mode, the number and share that did not conform to the applicable qualification, certification-tier, or mode requirements, the number of findings under subsection (e), and the number of orders withheld for failure to satisfy that subsection. Where more than one interpreter or mode was used, each shall be reported without counting the hearing more than once in the total or in the non-conforming share. The publication shall identify the applicable requirement, each recorded exception and its basis, and any missing record or recording; an undocumented exception shall not be counted as compliance. A hearing or oral interview without a record sufficient to establish compliance shall be counted as non-conforming.\n\nFor this publication, a **court** means each immigration court at each location, separately identifying an appellate body and each location at which it conducts hearings. For asylum and officer proceedings, the publication shall provide the same measures separately for each office and location and each language, applying only the requirements that govern those proceedings. The first publication covers the part of the calendar year beginning on the date in section 606(f)(2).\n\n**(c) Staffing statement to Congress.** Where a court's share of non-conforming hearings in a calendar year exceeds the share set in the schedule to this Act, **the publication shall state that the pattern is evidence of inadequate interpreter staffing and shall identify, by language and certification tier, the number of interpreters that court requires to conform to the law**. The responsible official shall transmit the statement to Congress and, for a court administered by an executive agency, to the head of that agency, and shall renew it annually until the pattern ends. The statement shall identify the staffing calculation and the hearing volume and scheduling assumptions used. A recital that resources are needed does not satisfy this subsection. The court-wide share is the number of hearings at which interpretation was required that were non-conforming, divided by all hearings at which interpretation was required; the publication shall also show the share for each language. A hearing whose record does not establish whether interpretation was required shall be included in both the numerator and the denominator as non-conforming. This subsection applies to each office and location of the Department of Homeland Security for which measures are published under subsection (b) as it applies to a court.\n\n**(d) Annual audit of recordings.** The Inspector General of the Department of Justice shall annually audit interpretation in proceedings before immigration judges and the appellate bodies and reviewers described in section 601(a) that are conducted within that Department, and the Comptroller General of the United States shall annually audit interpretation in proceedings of the United States Immigration Court. The Inspector General of the Department of Homeland Security shall annually audit interpretation in that Department's proceedings covered by this Title. Each auditor named in this subsection shall independently select, by a random method published in advance and from a seed published after the audited period closes, so that any person can reproduce the selection, a sample of hearing and oral-interview recordings representing each court or office, each language, certification tiers, modes, and findings of unavailability, compare the recordings with the records and publications required by this section, and publish the method, sample coverage, findings, missing recordings, and discrepancies. An audit shall examine whether interpretation was supplied as recorded, whether exchanges were left uninterpreted, whether interpretation accurately conveyed their content, and whether each recorded basis of qualification is verifiable. An audit may not review the merits of an immigration decision or the deliberations of a judge.\n\nThe officials named in subsection (a) shall ensure that every hearing or oral interview at which interpretation is required is recorded in a form preserving both the original speech and its interpretation, shall preserve the recordings and associated records for 10 years, and shall furnish them to the auditor on request. An assertion that recordings are unavailable does not satisfy the audit duty; the audit shall identify each such failure. There are authorized to be appropriated such sums as are necessary to carry out the recording, publication, and audit duties of this section.\n\n**(e) The record before an order is entered.** In a proceeding brought within section 1827(j) of title 28 by section 601(a), **no order shall be entered following a hearing or oral interview at which interpretation was required unless the record shows that a certified interpreter was used, or contains a finding that no certified interpreter in the language could be scheduled**. For multiple hearings, interviews, or languages, the record shall establish the condition for each. The finding shall identify the interpreters or sources contacted, when each was contacted, the scheduling alternatives considered, and the response received. A conclusion, a checked box, or a recital that none was available does not satisfy this subsection. Where the Director has not certified interpreters in the language under section 1827(b)(1) of title 28, a finding that so states satisfies this subsection, and identification of interpreters or sources contacted is not required. A finding is not sufficient where a certified interpreter in the language was available at another time at which the hearing or interview could lawfully have been held and it was not set for that time. The finding does not excuse use of an unqualified interpreter or alter any requirement of section 1827 of title 28. **This entry condition does not apply to officer proceedings.**"},{"anchor":"sec-608","heading":"\u00a7 608. Enforcement of interpretation and information duties","part":"TITLE VI \u2014 INTERPRETATION AND TRANSLATION","body":"**(a) Public access.** The officials responsible under section 607 shall disclose the data, records of certification tier and mode, findings of unavailability, staffing statements, and audit methods and findings required by that section, and shall permit audit of the sampled recordings, **upon request by members of the public**. Each member of the public has a right to receive the information and to inspect and audit the sampled recordings, independently of any right in an immigration proceeding. These rights permit evaluation of compliance, selection of counsel, informed participation in a person's own proceeding, and petitioning Congress and the responsible agency concerning interpreter staffing and practices.\n\nInformation disclosed publicly shall omit personal identifying information about persons in immigration proceedings and witnesses. Notwithstanding any law restricting disclosure of those proceedings, the custodian shall permit access to the sampled recordings sufficient to evaluate interpretation, using redaction only to the extent it does not prevent that evaluation and supervised access where necessary to preserve it. A requester given supervised access to identifying information shall not disclose that information. **Confidentiality does not permit withholding the data or denying an effective audit of a sampled recording.** Publication does not satisfy a request for data or access not supplied by that publication. A refusal, failure to supply the requested information or access, or offer of access that prevents the audit is a denial.\n\n**(b) Action for denied information.** Any person aggrieved by a denial under subsection (a), including an organization, may bring an action against the responsible official in the United States district court for the district where the records are maintained or for the District of Columbia. The court shall have jurisdiction to determine the denial de novo and, on finding a denial, shall order disclosure or access. The action supplies disclosure and audit access only; it does not authorize an order compelling compliance with interpretation requirements in another person's proceeding or review of an individual removal order. No administrative appeal or exhaustion is required.\n\n**(c) Prospective compliance.** A membership organization may bring an action in the United States district court for the district where the challenged practice operates or for the District of Columbia against the official responsible for a court-level or agency-level practice that violates this Title, **on behalf of an identified member with a pending proceeding who faces a sufficiently imminent and substantial risk of denial of interpretation or translation required by this Title**. The organization shall identify the member's court or forum and language and a scheduled or otherwise imminent hearing or proceeding step to which the challenged practice applies. The member may be identified to the court under seal and may proceed under a pseudonym. Where an identified member's proceeding step occurs or the member's proceeding ends after the action is filed, the organization may substitute another identified member, the action is not moot as to a class or as to other members, and a class claim relates back to the filing of the complaint. The interests protected shall be germane to the organization's purposes. A membership organization means an organization whose members joined voluntarily, or whose represented constituents select its governing body, are eligible to serve in it, and finance its activities. Serving clients alone does not establish membership.\n\nAny person with a pending proceeding who faces that risk, including a member, may bring the same action on the person's own behalf, including through counsel supplied by an organization. **On finding that a practice violates this Title and that a plaintiff or identified member faces the risk described in this subsection, the court shall grant prospective declaratory and injunctive relief to require compliance with this Title, including section 1827 of title 28 as extended by section 601(a), against the challenged scheduling, staffing, certification, mode, or translation practice.** Denial of interpretation or translation required by this Title is irreparable injury, and compliance with this Title is in the public interest. Relief may extend to the organization's members, multiple plaintiffs, or a class certified under Rule 23 of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure. Relief against a practice may benefit every person subject to it. This action reviews no individual removal order, reopens no proceeding, and provides no damages. Relief shall address prospective compliance and shall not vacate or stay an entered removal order. Individual participation needed to establish a member's standing does not authorize adjudication of individual removability or relief in the action.\n\n**(d) Jurisdiction and immunities.** Sections 1252(a)(2)(A), (a)(5), (b)(9), (e)(1), (e)(3), and (g) of title 8, and any other provision of that section limiting jurisdiction, venue, time to sue, or relief, do not channel, preclude, or bar an action under this section; this exception confers no authority to review an individual removal order. **The United States expressly waives sovereign immunity for the declaratory and injunctive relief and disclosure or audit access authorized by this section**, including an action against an official of the United States Immigration Court in the official's official capacity to perform a duty under this Title. No judicial or other official immunity bars that prospective relief. Each cause of action is statutory, and the existence of another remedial scheme does not displace it. No administrative remedy is a prerequisite.\n\n**(e) Exception to the bar on injunctive relief.** *Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1252(f)(3).*\n\n> \"(3) Paragraph (1) shall not apply to an action brought pursuant to section 1225(b)(3) of this title, subsections (e) or (f) of section 1226 of this title, ~~or~~ section 1231(a)(2)(B) of this title**, or section 608(c) of the Day in Court Act**.\"\n\n**(f) Fee awards.** An amount awarded or paid as attorney's fees, expert fees, or costs under a judgment or court-approved settlement in an action under this section is not included in the gross income of the person on whose behalf the action was brought, is payable directly to the counsel or organization that represented the prevailing party, and is not subject to administrative offset or levy for any debt of that party."},{"anchor":"sec-701","heading":"\u00a7 701. Establishment","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"**(a)** There is established, under article I, section 8, clause 9 of the Constitution, a court of record to be known as the **United States Immigration Court.**\n\n**(b) Not within any department.** The Court is not an agency, component, or instrumentality of the Department of Justice, of the Department of Homeland Security, or of any executive department, and **is not subject to the direction or supervision of any officer of the executive branch.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-702","heading":"\u00a7 702. Composition; where the Court sits","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"**(a) Judges.** The Court consists of such number of judges as the Congress provides, and of the Appellate Division established by \u00a7 705.\n\n**(b) One court, sitting regionally.** The Court is a single court. It shall sit at such places throughout the United States as the caseload requires, including at facilities in which persons are detained, and **a judge sitting in any place exercises the jurisdiction of the whole Court.** Divisions of the Court are administrative and do not divide its jurisdiction or its law.\n\n**(c) Chief Judge.** The judges shall elect a Chief Judge from among themselves for a term set in the schedule to this Act. **The Chief Judge is elected by the judges and is not designated by any officer outside the Court.**"},{"anchor":"sec-703","heading":"\u00a7 703. Appointment, term, and removal","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"**(a) Appointment.** Judges of the Court are appointed by the President, by and with the advice and consent of the Senate.\n\n**(b) Term.** A judge serves for a term of years set in the schedule to this Act, and is eligible for reappointment.\n\n**(c) Removal.** A judge may be removed by the President **only for inefficiency, neglect of duty, or malfeasance in office**, and only after notice of the grounds and an opportunity to be heard on the record. **The grounds shall be published.** A judge may not be removed, suspended, reassigned, or have compensation reduced by reason of a decision, a ruling, or the outcome of any case.\n\n**(d) Compensation.** The compensation of a judge shall not be diminished during the term."},{"anchor":"sec-704","heading":"\u00a7 704. Jurisdiction","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"**(a)** The Court has jurisdiction over proceedings under section 1229a of title 8, over custody and bond proceedings, and over such other proceedings as are by law assigned to an immigration judge.\n\n**(b) Transferred.** The functions of immigration judges under title 8 are transferred to the Court. **\u00a7 706 amends the definition of \"immigration judge\" accordingly**, and that amendment governs the meaning of the term wherever it appears."},{"anchor":"sec-705","heading":"\u00a7 705. Appellate Division","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"**(a) Establishment.** There is established within the Court an **Appellate Division**, which exercises the functions previously exercised by the Board of Immigration Appeals.\n\n**(b) Decisions are the law of the Court.** A published decision of the Appellate Division binds every judge of the Court until modified by the Appellate Division sitting en banc or by a court of appeals.\n\n**(c) No review outside the Court.** A decision of the Appellate Division is reviewable **only as \u00a7 708 provides.** No officer of the executive branch may review, take on certification to that officer, vacate, or modify a decision of the Court or of its Appellate Division. **This subsection does not limit the exercise of discretion not to execute a removal order, to grant parole, to defer action, or to decline to pursue a case** \u2014 a decision not to enforce is not a review of the Court's decision, and nothing in this Title compels any enforcement action."},{"anchor":"sec-706","heading":"\u00a7 706. The Attorney General's role ends","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1101(b)(4).*\n\n> \"The term 'immigration judge' means ~~an attorney whom the Attorney General appoints as an administrative judge within the Executive Office for Immigration Review, qualified to conduct specified classes of proceedings, including a hearing under section 1229a of this title. An immigration judge shall be subject to such supervision and shall perform such duties as the Attorney General shall prescribe, but shall not be employed by the Immigration and Naturalization Service.~~ **a judge of the United States Immigration Court established under Title VII of the Day in Court Act.**\"\n\n**(b) Certification abolished.** The authority of the Attorney General to direct that a case be referred to the Attorney General for review, or to decide a case so referred, **is abolished.** Any regulation providing for such referral has no effect.\n\n**(c) Legal positions.** The Attorney General may state the position of the United States in a proceeding before the Court as a party may, **and in no other manner.** A statement so made binds no judge of the Court."},{"anchor":"sec-707","heading":"\u00a7 707. Decisional independence","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"**(a) No quotas.** **No numerical case-completion quota, disposal rate, or time-to-completion metric shall be applied to a judge of the Court as a condition of appointment or reappointment, a measure of performance, or a basis for removal, reassignment, or any other personnel action.** The Court may set its own docketing practices, may publish aggregate statistics on its caseload, and may report to the Congress; **it shall not make an individual judge's tenure or assignment depend on a rate of disposal.**\n\n**(b) No direction as to outcome.** No person shall direct, request, or attempt to influence a judge as to the outcome of, or the reasoning in, a particular case. This subsection does not limit argument by a party on the record.\n\n**(c) Support.** The Court shall have such clerks, interpreters, and administrative personnel as it requires, appointed by and responsible to the Court."},{"anchor":"sec-707a","heading":"\u00a7 707A. Cases are decided by judges","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"**(a) Definition.** In this Section, **\"automated system\"** means a computational process that produces an output bearing on the disposition of a proceeding or on a fact at issue in one, whatever the method by which the output is produced, whether or not the process is described as artificial intelligence, and whether or not a person could produce the same output by other means.\n\n**(b) A person decides.** Every order, finding of fact, conclusion of law, determination of credibility, grant or denial of relief or protection, custody or bond determination, and order of removal in a proceeding before the Court **shall be made by a judge of the Court, who is a natural person**, and shall be entered as the act of that judge. **No automated system may make any such decision**, and the output of an automated system is not an order, finding, conclusion, determination, or decision.\n\n**(c) Credibility.** **No automated system may produce an output assessing the credibility, truthfulness, or demeanor of a witness**, and no such output may be furnished to the Court, received in evidence, or considered for any purpose. **The credibility of a person's account is the determination on which relief most often turns, is made from the witness before the judge, and is committed to the judge alone.**\n\n**(d) What a system may do.** Nothing in this Section limits the use of an automated system to docket, index, search, retrieve, organize, transcribe, summarize, calculate, sort, cite-check, format, schedule, notify, or draft, or to perform any other function in the administration of the Court or in the preparation of a decision. **Interpretation and translation are governed by Title VI**, and nothing in this subsection authorizes an automated system to perform a function that Title VI requires of a qualified interpreter or translator.\n\n**(e) No delegation, and no inquiry into deliberation.** The disposition of a proceeding may not be delegated to an automated system by rule, practice, assignment, or consent. A decision prepared with the assistance of an automated system is the act of the judge who enters it. **This Section states who decides.** It creates no ground to inquire into how a judge reached a decision and no right in any party to discovery or disclosure concerning it.\n\n**(f) Immigration judges however constituted.** Until the transfer under \u00a7 704(b) takes effect, and if this Title is held invalid, this Section applies to every immigration judge however constituted and to every officer exercising the functions of an immigration judge.\n\n**(g) Relation to the Accountable Government Act.** An order of removal is an individualized adverse action within \u00a7 2-1(a) of the Accountable Government Act, so **\u00a7 2-2B of that Act reaches every such decision made by an officer or employee of an agency** \u2014 including a determination under Title VIII of this Act and, before the transfer under \u00a7 704(b), a decision of an immigration judge. On and after that transfer the judges of the Court exercise no executive power and administer no covered authority, as \u00a7 710(a) provides, so that Act does not reach the Court and **this Section governs alone.** Where both apply, they operate together and neither displaces the other.\n\n**(h) Severability.** Subsection (c) is severable from the remainder of this Section. If any subsection is held invalid, every other continues to operate, and if this Section is held invalid as applied to the Court it continues to operate under subsection (f)."},{"anchor":"sec-708","heading":"\u00a7 708. Review unchanged","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"**(a)** A final decision of the Appellate Division is reviewable on petition for review in the court of appeals for the circuit in which the proceeding was conducted, **as section 1252 of title 8 provides.**\n\n**(b) No specialized appellate court.** Nothing in this Title creates, and this Title shall not be construed to create, exclusive appellate jurisdiction over immigration matters in any single court. **Review remains distributed among the regional courts of appeals**."},{"anchor":"sec-709","heading":"\u00a7 709. Transition","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"**(a) Reserved for the drafting committee.** The transfer of pending cases, the conversion or appointment of persons serving as immigration judges and members of the Board of Immigration Appeals on the effective date, the treatment of decisions of the Board issued before that date, the transfer of records, personnel, and appropriations, and the effective date of this Title, are **reserved.**\n\n**(b) What the transition may not do.** No transition provision shall subject a judge of the Court to supervision by an officer of the executive branch, permit review of a decision of the Court by such an officer, or make the tenure of a judge depend on a case outcome.\n\n**(c) Continuity of proceedings.** No proceeding pending on the effective date abates, and no deadline, application, or form of relief is lost by reason of the transfer."},{"anchor":"sec-710","heading":"\u00a7 710. Construction","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"**(a) Judicial power, not executive power.** The judges of the Court exercise the judicial power of a court established under article I. **They exercise no executive power**, and the removal protection in \u00a7 703(c) is a term of judicial office of the kind Congress has long provided for the judges of the Tax Court and of the Court of Appeals for Veterans Claims.\n\n**(b) Substantive law unchanged.** This Title alters no ground of inadmissibility or removal, no form of relief, no burden or standard of proof, and no substantive rule of immigration law.\n\n**(c) Severability.** If \u00a7 703(c) is held invalid, every other provision of this Title remains in force and the Court continues to exist. **If this Title is held invalid in whole, Titles I through VI are unaffected** and continue to apply to proceedings before immigration judges however constituted."},{"anchor":"sec-711","heading":"\u00a7 711. Timeliness","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"**(a) Legal sufficiency, decided first.** Where a claim is subject to \u00a7 802(e), the Court shall make that determination **within 30 days** of the proceeding being commenced. A claim not dismissed under \u00a7 802(e) proceeds under subsection (b).\n\n**(b) The ordinary period.** The Court shall decide a claim for protection under section 1158 of title 8, section 1231(b)(3) of that title, or the Convention Against Torture **within 90 days** of the proceeding being commenced.\n\n**(c) Extension for complexity.** The period in subsection (b) is extended to **180 days** only on a determination, recorded before the ordinary period expires and stating the facts particular to the case, that the case requires additional time because it presents one or more of the following:\n\n1. a contested question whether a criminal conviction is a ground of removal or a bar to relief;\n2. country-conditions evidence or expert testimony that cannot reasonably be assembled within the ordinary period;\n3. a question of competency under \u00a7 503(e), or a person under 18 years of age;\n4. a claim involving more than one family member whose proceedings are consolidated;\n5. evidence in the possession of the Government that has been requested and not produced; or\n6. the unavailability of counsel or of a qualified interpreter, **where the unavailability is not attributable to the person.**\n\n**(d) Beyond 180 days.** A proceeding may continue beyond 180 days only on a **further determination at each 60-day interval**, stating what remains to be done and why, **published in the aggregate under subsection (f).** No determination under this subsection may rest on the Court's caseload; that circumstance is addressed by subsection (g) and \u00a7 712.\n\n**(e) The clock is the Government's to lose, not the person's.** No period in this Section runs against a person, and **no claim is dismissed, waived, prejudiced, or presumed denied by reason of any period expiring.** A period is tolled only for a delay the person requested or caused, recorded as such.\n\n**(f) Published.** The Court shall publish annually, **by location and by track** \u2014\n1. the number of \u00a7 802(e) determinations made within 30 days and after;\n2. the number of claims decided within 90 days, within 180 days, and after;\n3. the number extended under subsection (c), **by the ground relied upon**;\n4. the number continued beyond 180 days, and the median and 90th-percentile time to decision;\n5. **continuances granted, by the party requesting and by the reason**; and\n6. the number of cases in which evidence requested from the Government was not produced before the hearing.\n\n**(f-1) And the shortfall is named for what it is.** Where the numbers published under subsection (f) show claims decided after the applicable period in more than the share set in the schedule to this Act, **that pattern is evidence that the Court's adjudicative capacity is inadequate and shall be stated as such in the publication.**\n\n**(g) Not a quota on any judge.** This Section states a duty of **the Court as an institution.** It is not a case-completion quota, and **\u00a7 707(a) applies to it in full** \u2014 no judge's tenure, assignment, or evaluation may depend on compliance with this Section.\n\n**(h) Where the periods cannot be met.** Where the Court cannot meet the periods in this Section, the Chief Judge shall certify to the Congress **the number of judges, interpreters, counsel and support personnel required to do so**, and shall renew the certification annually until they are met."},{"anchor":"sec-712","heading":"\u00a7 712. Resources","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"**(a) Judgeships follow the certification.** There are authorized such judgeships of the Court as the Chief Judge certifies under \u00a7 711(h) to be required, and the Court shall seat each judge appointed to one. Appointment is made as \u00a7 703(a) provides, and nothing in this subsection appoints any person or directs any nomination.\n\n**(b) Permanent appropriation for adjudication.** There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary for **the compensation of judges of the Court and of counsel appointed under Title V**. Where section 606(e) is not in force, this appropriation also covers the compensation of interpreters and translators required by Title VI. **Section 606(e) is the primary appropriation for those costs while it is in force, and no cost may be paid from both appropriations.** Amounts under this subsection are appropriated permanently and indefinitely, are available without further appropriation and without fiscal-year limitation, and do not lapse. **The adjudicative capacity of the Court does not depend on an annual appropriation**.\n\n**(c) Authorization for the remainder.** There are authorized to be appropriated such sums as are necessary for every other purpose of this Act, including case management, electronic filing, records production under \u00a7 713(d), and the programs required by \u00a7 304A.\n\n**(d) No displacement.** Amounts appropriated under this Section are in addition to, and shall not be derived from, amounts otherwise available for the enforcement of the immigration laws. **Resourcing adjudication shall not be accomplished by reducing anything else, and reducing enforcement is not a purpose of this Act.**\n\n**(e) Continuity.** The conduct of pending proceedings, the compensation of appointed counsel, and the provision of interpretation are activities authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations, on the terms \u00a7 507(b) provides."},{"anchor":"sec-713","heading":"\u00a7 713. Measures to expedite","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"**(a) An officer may grant, and may not deny.** An asylum officer may **grant** asylum, withholding, or protection under the Convention Against Torture in the first instance, and a grant so made concludes the proceeding. **An officer may not deny, dismiss, screen, or decline to refer a claim.** Where an officer does not grant, the officer **takes no decision on the claim at all** \u2014 the claim proceeds before the Court under \u00a7 802, and **the officer's non-grant is not a determination, is not evidence, and shall not be communicated to the Court.** **The prohibition in \u00a7 802(g) is a prohibition on refusing relief, not on affording it**.\n\n**(b) Issue narrowing.** The Court shall hold a pre-hearing conference in each proceeding expected to require a hearing, at which the parties shall identify the issues genuinely in dispute and stipulate to those that are not. **A matter not disputed at the conference is not to be relitigated at the hearing.**\n\n**(c) Stipulated relief.** Where the Government does not oppose relief, the Court may grant it on the papers without a hearing.\n\n**(d) The record, before the hearing.** The Government shall produce to the person and to counsel, **not later than 30 days before the hearing**, the complete record on which it relies, including the administrative file. **A hearing shall not be held earlier than 30 days after production unless the person elects otherwise on the record**, and a continuance made necessary by late production is not attributable to the person for purposes of \u00a7 711(e). **This subsection does not apply to a determination under \u00a7 802(e)**, which is made on the account the person gives and requires no record from the Government.\n\n**(e) Continuances.** A party seeking a continuance shall state the reason on the record, and the Court shall record the party and the reason. **\u00a7 711(f)(5) publishes them.**\n\n**(f) The docket belongs to the Court.** No officer or employee outside the Court shall **direct the order in which the Court hears cases, prioritize any class of case, assign any case to any judge, or condition any resource or approval upon any of those things.** A party may move to advance or continue a particular case on the record, and any person may request expedition of their own case; **what this subsection forbids is the reordering of the docket as a matter of policy from outside the Court.**\n\n**(g) Appearance and filing.** The Court shall permit electronic filing and appearance by video or telephone at the election of the person, **and shall not require a person to appear in person where the person elects otherwise**, except where the Court finds on the record that physical presence is necessary.\n\n**(h) Consolidation.** Proceedings involving members of one family unit shall be consolidated and heard together unless a party shows that consolidation would prejudice a claim."},{"anchor":"sec-714","heading":"\u00a7 714. Employment authorization follows the delay","part":"TITLE VII \u2014 THE UNITED STATES IMMIGRATION COURT","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1158(d)(2).*\n\n> \"An applicant for asylum is not entitled to employment authorization, but such authorization may be provided under regulation by the Attorney General. An applicant who is not otherwise eligible for employment authorization shall not be granted such authorization ~~prior to 180 days after the date of filing of the application for asylum~~ **while the application is pending within the periods established by section 711 of the Day in Court Act. Where a decision has not been made within the period applicable under that section, including any extension recorded under it, employment authorization shall be granted, and no application, fee, or discretionary determination is required. In no event shall authorization be withheld beyond 180 days after the date of filing of the application. Authorization under this paragraph arises by operation of law upon the expiry of the applicable period; the Secretary of Homeland Security shall issue documentary evidence of it not later than 15 days thereafter; and the failure to issue that evidence neither delays nor affects the authorization.**\"\n\n**(c) Once vested, it continues.** Authorization granted under subsection (a) continues **while an appeal to the Appellate Division or a timely petition for review is pending**, and is not withdrawn by reason of a decision on the claim while such review remains available. **It ends when the claim is finally resolved against the person and no review remains available**, or on the person's departure or removal.\n\n**(d) Delay the applicant caused.** A period is not missed for purposes of subsection (a) to the extent it was tolled under \u00a7 711(e) for a delay the applicant requested or caused. **Tolling requires a contemporaneous record**, and a period not recorded as tolled is not tolled.\n\n**(e) Grants unaffected.** A person granted asylum, withholding, or protection under the Convention Against Torture is authorized to work as existing law provides, without regard to this Section.\n\n**(f) No inference against those who work.** Nothing in this Section, and no absence of authorization under it, bears on any protection a person has under the labor and employment laws of the United States or of any State. **A person who works without authorization retains every protection those laws afford**, and this Section creates no defense for an employer."},{"anchor":"sec-801","heading":"\u00a7 801. The authority confined","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 EXPEDITED REMOVAL","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1225(b)(1)(A)(iii).*\n\n> **(iii) Application to certain other aliens**\n>\n> **(I) In general.** ~~The Attorney General may apply clauses (i) and (ii) of this subparagraph to any or all aliens described in subclause (II) as designated by the Attorney General. Such designation shall be in the sole and unreviewable discretion of the Attorney General and may be modified at any time.~~ **Clauses (i) and (ii) apply only to an alien described in subclause (II). No officer may extend the application of those clauses beyond that subclause by designation or otherwise, and no designation is required for them to apply within it.**\n>\n> **(II) Aliens described.** ~~An alien described in this clause is an alien who is not described in subparagraph (F), who has not been admitted or paroled into the United States, and who has not affirmatively shown, to the satisfaction of an immigration officer, that the alien has been physically present in the United States continuously for the 2-year period immediately prior to the date of the determination of inadmissibility under this subparagraph.~~ **An alien described in this clause is an alien who is not described in subparagraph (F), who has not been admitted or paroled, who is encountered at a port of entry or within the period after entry set in the schedule to the Day in Court Act, and who has not affirmatively shown that the alien has been physically present in the United States continuously for that period. The burden of establishing that an alien is described in this clause is on the Government.**\n\n**(b) The showing is not the officer's to withhold.** The former requirement that presence be shown *\"to the satisfaction of an immigration officer\"* is struck. **Whether an alien is described in subclause (II) is a question of fact reviewable under section 1252(e)(2) as amended by \u00a7 803.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE VIII \u2014 EXPEDITED REMOVAL","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-802","heading":"\u00a7 802. A claim of fear removes the person from this Section","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 EXPEDITED REMOVAL","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1225(b)(1)(A)(ii).*\n\n> **(ii) Claims for asylum.** \"If an immigration officer determines that an alien \u2026 is inadmissible \u2026 and the alien indicates either an intention to apply for asylum under section 1158 of this title~~, or a fear of persecution~~ **, a fear of persecution, a fear of torture, or a fear of return**, the officer shall ~~refer the alien for an interview by an asylum officer under subparagraph (B)~~ **place the alien in a proceeding under section 1229a of this title before the United States Immigration Court, and this paragraph shall not thereafter apply to that alien**.\"\n\n**(b) What triggers it.** A claim under subsection (a) is made by **any indication of fear of return, in any language, in any form, to any officer or employee** \u2014 orally, in writing, or by conduct a reasonable officer would understand as expressing fear. **No particular words are required**, and the alien need not name a statute or a form of relief. **No officer may assess the merit, plausibility, or credibility of the claim** for purposes of this Section \u2014 the officer's only question is whether fear was indicated. **The officer shall record what was said or done and the date**, and where the officer determines that no fear was indicated, shall record that determination and the basis for it. **An unrecorded determination that no fear was indicated is not a determination** for purposes of section 1252(e)(2)(E).\n\n**(c) The referral is not a screen.** Subparagraph (B) and the credible-fear procedure do not apply to an alien described in subsection (a). **The question whether the fear is well founded is decided by the Court in the proceeding**, with counsel under Title V and interpretation under Title VI.\n\n**(d) One-way.** Once \u00a7 1229a applies to an alien under this Section, **no officer may return the alien to processing under section 1225(b)(1)**, and no withdrawal, recantation, or later statement removes the alien from the Court's jurisdiction.\n\n**(e) Legal sufficiency, determined by the Court.** In a proceeding to which this Section applies, **the Court may dismiss a claim for protection on the papers where the account the person gives, taken as true, identifies no ground cognizable** under section 1158, section 1231(b)(3), or the Convention Against Torture. **The account is taken as true for this purpose.** This is a determination of legal sufficiency and **is not a determination of credibility, plausibility, or weight**, and no such determination shall be made under this subsection.\n\n**(f) What the screen requires.** A dismissal under subsection (e) may be made only where the person has had **counsel under Title V and interpretation under Title VI**, has been told in a language they understand what ground the account fails to identify, and has been **given one opportunity to amend.** A dismissal that does not identify the missing ground does not satisfy this subsection.\n\n**(g) An officer never applies this Section.** Subsections (e) and (f) are exercised **by the Court and by no other body.** No immigration officer, asylum officer, or officer of any department may dismiss, screen, or decline to refer a claim on any ground, and **\u00a7 802(a) operates without regard to any officer's view of the claim.**\n\n**(h) Reviewable.** A dismissal under subsection (e) is a final order for purposes of section 1252 of title 8, and is reviewable **de novo** under Title X."},{"anchor":"sec-803","heading":"\u00a7 803. Judicial review restored","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 EXPEDITED REMOVAL","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1252(e)(2), and the three further provisions that would otherwise bar the claim it creates: \u00a7 1252(a)(2)(A)(iii), \u00a7 1252(e)(5), and \u00a7 1252(e)(4).*\n\n**(a) What may be determined.**\n\n*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1252(e)(2).*\n\n> \"Judicial review of any determination made under section 1225(b)(1) of this title is available in habeas corpus proceedings, ~~but shall be limited to determinations of\u2014~~ **and extends to\u2014**\n>\n> (A) whether the petitioner is an alien,\n>\n> (B) whether the petitioner was ordered removed under such section, ~~and~~\n>\n> (C) whether the petitioner can prove by a preponderance of the evidence that the petitioner is an alien lawfully admitted for permanent residence, has been admitted as a refugee under section 1157 of this title, or has been granted asylum under section 1158 of this title, such status not having been terminated, and is entitled to such further inquiry as prescribed by the Attorney General pursuant to section 1225(b)(1)(C) of this title **,**\n>\n> **(D) whether the petitioner is an alien described in section 1225(b)(1)(A)(iii)(II) of this title, on which the Government bears the burden, and**\n>\n> **(E) whether the petitioner indicated a fear within the meaning of section 1225(b)(1)(A)(ii) of this title.**\"\n\n**(b) The unqualified clause is qualified.**\n\n*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1252(a)(2)(A)(iii).*\n\n> \"(iii) **except as provided in subsection (e),** the application of such section to individual aliens, including the determination made under section 1225(b)(1)(B) of this title, or\"\n\n**Nothing else in subparagraph (A) changes.** **The bar on reviewing the application of \u00a7 1225(b)(1) to individual aliens is retained in full as to everything subsection (e) does not authorize** \u2014 the amendment opens clause (iii) to subsection (e) and to nothing else.\n\n**(c) The scope of inquiry.**\n\n*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1252(e)(5) by adding at the end.*\n\n> \"In determining whether an alien has been ordered removed under section 1225(b)(1) of this title, the court's inquiry shall be limited to whether such an order in fact was issued and whether it relates to the petitioner. There shall be no review of whether the alien is actually inadmissible or entitled to any relief from removal. **This paragraph does not limit a determination under subparagraph (D) or (E) of paragraph (2), and on such a determination the court may receive and consider evidence that is not in the administrative record.**\"\n\n**(d) The remedy.**\n\n*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1252(e)(4).*\n\n> \"In any case where the court determines that the petitioner\u2014\n>\n> (A) is an alien who was not ordered removed under section 1225(b)(1) of this title, ~~or~~\n>\n> (B) has demonstrated by a preponderance of the evidence that the alien is an alien lawfully admitted for permanent residence, has been admitted as a refugee under section 1157 of this title, or has been granted asylum under section 1158 of this title **,**\n>\n> **(C) is not an alien described in section 1225(b)(1)(A)(iii)(II) of this title, or**\n>\n> **(D) indicated a fear within the meaning of section 1225(b)(1)(A)(ii) of this title,**\n>\n> the court may order no remedy or relief other than to require that the petitioner be provided a hearing in accordance with section 1229a of this title **and, on a determination under subparagraph (C) or (D), to vacate the order of removal.** Any alien who is provided a hearing under section 1229a of this title pursuant to this paragraph may thereafter obtain judicial review of any resulting final order of removal pursuant to subsection (a)(1).\"\n\n**(e) Relief.** Where a court determines that the petitioner is not described in section 1225(b)(1)(A)(iii)(II), or indicated a fear within section 1225(b)(1)(A)(ii), **the order of removal is vacated** and the alien is placed in a proceeding under section 1229a. **Release from custody is not the only relief available on such a petition**, and the availability of any other relief is not a ground to deny the writ.\n\n**(f) Section 1252(e)(1)(A) is satisfied, not evaded.** That subparagraph bars equitable relief in an action pertaining to a \u00a7 1225(b)(1) order **\"except as specifically authorized in a subsequent paragraph of this subsection.\"** The vacatur provided by subsection (d) of this Section is authorized in paragraph (4) \u2014 a subsequent paragraph \u2014 and is therefore within the exception on the face of the existing text. **No amendment of paragraph (e)(1) is required, and none is made.**\n\n**(g) Congress's determination.** The limits amended by this Section were enacted by the Congress and are **amended by the Congress**. **The omission from paragraph (2), from paragraph (4), and from clause (a)(2)(A)(iii) of any limitation appearing elsewhere in section 1252 is a determination by Congress that the limitation does not apply to a determination under subparagraph (D) or (E) of paragraph (2)**, and is not a gap to be filled by reference to that section. Nothing in this Section rests on, or states a view about, the requirements of the Suspension Clause."},{"anchor":"sec-804","heading":"\u00a7 804. Physical presence controls","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 EXPEDITED REMOVAL","body":"**(a)** For purposes of this Act, a person **physically present in the United States is within the United States**, without regard to whether the person has been admitted, has effected an entry, is treated by any other provision of law as an applicant for admission or as not having entered, is paroled, or is apprehended near a border or shortly after arrival.\n\n**(b) The fiction is not imported.** No doctrine treating a person physically present as though standing outside the United States applies to any right, duty, or proceeding under this Act. **This subsection states the rule for this Act only**, and takes no position on the construction of any constitutional provision."},{"anchor":"sec-805","heading":"\u00a7 805. Construction","part":"TITLE VIII \u2014 EXPEDITED REMOVAL","body":"**(a) Anti-substitution.** Where an authority other than section 1225(b)(1) is invoked to remove **an alien who has indicated a fear within the meaning of section 1225(b)(1)(A)(ii)** without a proceeding under section 1229a, **the conditions of this Title apply to that authority according to their terms.** The agency shall publish annually the authorities so invoked and the number of removals effected under each. **This subsection does not reach a departure the alien requests**, including voluntary departure or withdrawal of an application for admission, provided the request is recorded, was made in a language the person understands as section 602(d) defines that term, and was not conditioned on forgoing a claim of fear.\n\n**(b) Nothing compelled.** This Title requires no person to be detained, removed, admitted, or granted any relief, and states no rule of decision on any application for asylum, withholding, or protection.\n\n**(c) Severability.** Each section is severable. **If \u00a7 801 is held invalid, \u00a7\u00a7 802 and 803 remain in force**; if \u00a7 803 is held invalid, the confinement in \u00a7 801 and the referral in \u00a7 802 are unaffected."},{"anchor":"sec-901","heading":"\u00a7 901. Actual receipt is a condition of the authority","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 NOTICE AND ORDERS IN ABSENTIA","body":"**(a) The condition.** **An order of removal shall not be entered against a person who does not appear unless the record establishes that the person actually received notice** of the time and place of the proceeding and of the consequences of failing to appear.\n\n**(b) What establishes actual receipt.** Actual receipt is established by personal service, by a signed receipt, by the person's or counsel's acknowledgment, by appearance at a prior hearing at which the time and place of the next was stated on the record in a language the person understands, or by any other evidence that the notice reached the person. **Proof of mailing is not proof of receipt.**\n\n**(c) Language.** Notice satisfies this Section only if furnished in a language the person understands, as \u00a7 602(b) requires. **Notice in a language the person does not understand is not notice.**\n\n**(d) Counsel.** Where the person is represented, notice shall be furnished to counsel as well, and **notice to the person alone does not satisfy this Section** where counsel of record has appeared.\n\n**(e) Refusal and evasion.** A person who **refuses service**, who is served personally and declines to accept the document, or who **conceals an address for the purpose of avoiding notice**, has actually received notice for purposes of this Section, on the Government's showing of that conduct. **The condition is receipt, not cooperation** \u2014 this subsection reaches deliberate avoidance and nothing else.\n\n**(f) Change of address.** Nothing in this Title relieves a person of the obligation to provide and update an address under section 1229(a)(1)(F) of title 8, and **a failure to do so is relevant to whether conduct under subsection (e) occurred.**\n\n**(g) This Section conditions the authority to enter the order.** It states no rule of decision on removability or on any application for relief, and creates no cause of action.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IX \u2014 NOTICE AND ORDERS IN ABSENTIA","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-902","heading":"\u00a7 902. Service by mail","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 NOTICE AND ORDERS IN ABSENTIA","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1229(c).*\n\n> \"Service by mail under this section shall be sufficient if there is ~~proof of attempted delivery to the last address provided by the alien~~ **evidence that the notice was received by the alien or the alien's counsel of record. Proof of attempted delivery is not evidence of receipt.** Where service is made by mail, the Government shall retain the record of delivery, and **the absence of such a record is evidence that notice was not received.**\""},{"anchor":"sec-903","heading":"\u00a7 903. The deeming clause struck","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 NOTICE AND ORDERS IN ABSENTIA","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1229a(b)(5)(A) and (B).*\n\n> **(A) In general.** \"\u2026shall be ordered removed in absentia if the Service establishes by clear, unequivocal, and convincing evidence that the written notice was so provided **and was actually received as section 901 of the Day in Court Act requires,** and that the alien is removable\u2026 ~~The written notice by the Attorney General shall be considered sufficient for purposes of this subparagraph if provided at the most recent address provided under section 1229(a)(1)(F) of this title.~~\"\n>\n> **(B) ~~No notice if failure to provide address information~~ Where no address has been provided.** ~~No written notice shall be required under subparagraph (A) if the alien has failed to provide the address required under section 1229(a)(1)(F) of this title.~~ **Where an alien has not provided the address required under section 1229(a)(1)(F) of this title, the Government shall attempt notice by each of the following means for which it holds information in the record of the proceeding: an address, telephone number, or electronic address of the alien; counsel of record; a custodian; and a sponsor or other person who assumed responsibility for the alien. The Government shall record which means were attempted and the result, and **attempting each such means satisfies this requirement.** No order shall be entered under subparagraph (A) unless notice was actually received.** **This subparagraph does not apply where the alien was not informed of the requirement to provide an address in a language the alien understands.**\""},{"anchor":"sec-904","heading":"\u00a7 904. Rescission","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 NOTICE AND ORDERS IN ABSENTIA","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1229a(b)(5)(C).*\n\n> \"Such an order may be rescinded ~~only~~\u2014 (i) upon a motion to reopen filed within 180 days after the date of the order of removal if the alien demonstrates that the failure to appear was because of exceptional circumstances\u2026, or (ii) upon a motion to reopen filed at any time if the alien demonstrates that the alien did not receive notice\u2026 or the alien demonstrates that the alien was in Federal or State custody and the failure to appear was through no fault of the alien**, or (iii) upon a motion to reopen filed at any time where the record does not affirmatively establish actual receipt of notice as section 901 of the Day in Court Act requires**. The filing of the motion to reopen described in clause (i)**,** (ii) **or (iii)** shall stay the removal of the alien pending disposition of the motion\u2026\"\n\n**(b) Burden.** On a motion under clause (ii) or clause (iii), **the Government bears the burden of establishing actual receipt**, and the words \"the alien demonstrates\" in clause (ii) do not require the alien to prove non-receipt where the Government's record does not affirmatively establish receipt. **A person is not required to prove a negative about a mailing they did not receive.**\n\n**(c) No numerical limit.** A motion under clause (ii) or (iii) is not counted against any numerical limitation on motions to reopen, and **no time bar applies to it.**\n\n**(d) Departure does not bar it.** A motion under clause (ii) or (iii) may be filed from outside the United States, **and the person's absence is not a ground to deny it.** Where it is granted, the Secretary shall facilitate return as \u00a7 203 provides."},{"anchor":"sec-905","heading":"\u00a7 905. Construction","part":"TITLE IX \u2014 NOTICE AND ORDERS IN ABSENTIA","body":"**(a) The wrong is the absence of the record.** This Title asks whether notice was received and whether the Government recorded that it was. **It asks no court to determine why a person did not appear**, except as \u00a7 1229a(b)(5)(C)(i) already provides.\n\n**(b) Not a windfall.** Where actual receipt is established, this Title changes nothing: the order stands and the ordinary rules govern.\n\n**(c) Severability.** Each section is severable. **If \u00a7 903 is held invalid, \u00a7 901 continues to condition the authority**, and if \u00a7 901 is held invalid, the amendments made by \u00a7\u00a7 902 through 904 remain in force."},{"anchor":"sec-1001","heading":"\u00a7 1001. Claims under this Act are reviewable","part":"TITLE X \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW OF RIGHTS UNDER THIS ACT","body":"**(a) The claim.** *Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1252(a)(2) by adding at the end.*\n\n> **\"(E) Claims under the Day in Court Act.** Subparagraphs (B) and (C) do not preclude review of a claim that a right, duty, or condition established by the Day in Court Act was not satisfied, **including a question of fact material to such a claim**, and subparagraph (D) does not limit the review available under this subparagraph. **A court reviewing such a claim may determine any question of fact on which the claim depends**, on the administrative record and on such further evidence as the court receives concerning what the record does or does not contain.\"\n\n**(b) The scope and standard of review conformed.** *Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1252(b)(4) by amending the matter preceding subparagraph (A).*\n\n> \"~~Except as provided in paragraph (5)(B)\u2014~~ **Except as provided in paragraph (5)(B), and except as to a claim described in subsection (a)(2)(E) of this section\u2014**\"\n\n**What this does and does not reach.** Subparagraphs (A) and (B) of paragraph (4) cease to apply **to a claim under this Act, and to nothing else.** On every other question in the same petition \u2014 removability, eligibility, the exercise of discretion \u2014 the administrative record remains the record and the compelled-conclusion standard remains the standard. **A petition raising both is governed by both**, claim by claim, exactly as \u00a7 1005(a-1) provides for a claim that depends in part on this Act and in part on another ground. Subparagraphs (C) and (D) of paragraph (4) are **unaffected in every case**: this Act states no rule of decision on admissibility and none on discretionary relief.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE X \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW OF RIGHTS UNDER THIS ACT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1002","heading":"\u00a7 1002. Not consolidated away","part":"TITLE X \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW OF RIGHTS UNDER THIS ACT","body":"*Amends 8 U.S.C. \u00a7 1252(b)(9) by adding at the end.*\n\n> **\"This paragraph does not bar an action brought before a final order is entered to enforce a right, duty, or condition established by the Day in Court Act, where the relief sought is the provision of counsel, of interpretation, of notice, or of a proceeding before the United States Immigration Court.** A claim that such a right was denied **is not merged into review of a final order**. The rights named in this paragraph are rights to something that must occur during the proceeding, and **that is the whole of the test** \u2014 a court need not determine whether a later remedy would have sufficed.\""},{"anchor":"sec-1003","heading":"\u00a7 1003. Standard of review","part":"TITLE X \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW OF RIGHTS UNDER THIS ACT","body":"**(a) Questions of law**, including the construction of this Act, are reviewed **de novo**.\n\n**(b) Whether the record establishes a predicate.** Where this Act conditions an authority on a fact appearing in the record \u2014 actual receipt of notice, appointment or waiver of counsel, provision of interpretation, a recorded objective, membership in a class \u2014 **the reviewing court determines de novo whether the record establishes it.** The question is what the record contains, and no deference attaches to the agency's characterization of its own record.\n\n**(c) Everything else is unchanged.** The standard of review applicable to any other determination, including a discretionary determination and a finding of removability, **is unaffected by this Title.**"},{"anchor":"sec-1004","heading":"\u00a7 1004. What this Title does not do","part":"TITLE X \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW OF RIGHTS UNDER THIS ACT","body":"**(a) No general restoration.** This Title does not repeal, narrow, or displace subparagraph (B) or (C) of section 1252(a)(2) as to any claim other than one arising under this Act. **A person may not use this Title to obtain review of a discretionary determination**, and the characterization of a claim as arising under this Act does not make it so.\n\n**(b) No new remedy.** The relief available on review is the relief the reviewing court could otherwise grant, together with the vacatur and reopening this Act specifically provides. **This Title creates no cause of action and no damages remedy.**\n\n**(c) Classwide relief unaddressed.** This Title takes no position on section 1252(f)(1), and neither expands nor confirms any limitation on the authority of a court to grant relief to persons other than the parties before it."},{"anchor":"sec-1005","heading":"\u00a7 1005. Construction","part":"TITLE X \u2014 JUDICIAL REVIEW OF RIGHTS UNDER THIS ACT","body":"**(a) Congress's own limits.** The limitations amended by this Title were enacted by the Congress and are amended by the Congress. **Nothing in this Title rests on, or states a view about, the requirements of the Suspension Clause, of Article III, or of the Due Process Clause.**\n\n**(a-1) The exception is not to be read narrowly.** A claim arises under this Act for purposes of \u00a7 1001 **whenever the relief sought depends on whether a right, duty, or condition established by this Act was satisfied**, without regard to how the claim is captioned, whether other grounds are also asserted, or whether the same facts bear on a determination this Title leaves unreviewable. **The omission from \u00a7 1001 of any limitation appearing elsewhere in section 1252 is a determination by Congress that the limitation does not apply to such a claim**, and is not a gap to be filled by reference to that section. Where a claim depends in part on this Act and in part on another ground, **it is reviewable as to the part that depends on this Act.**\n\n**(b) Severability, and its direction.** Each section is severable. **If \u00a7 1002 is held invalid, \u00a7 1001 remains in force** and claims under this Act remain reviewable on petition for review of a final order. **If this Title is held invalid in whole, Titles I through IX are unaffected** \u2014 each states a condition on an authority, and a condition operates whether or not its breach is separately reviewable."},{"anchor":"sec-1101","heading":"\u00a7 1101. Qualified orders","part":"TITLE XI \u2014 FINALITY","body":"**(a) Definition.** A final order of removal is a **qualified order** if the record of the proceeding shows each of the following:\n\n1. **Counsel.** Where the person was entitled to appointed counsel under \u00a7 503, counsel was appointed and **appeared and participated at the hearing on the merits**, or counsel was waived under \u00a7 504. Where the person was not so entitled, the person **was given the list of available counsel required by section 1229(a)(1)(E) of title 8 in a language the person understands, was afforded not less than the period set in the schedule to this Act to obtain counsel before the hearing on the merits, and received the record under \u00a7 713(d)** \u2014 and where the person appeared without counsel, the Court advised the person on the record of the right to obtain it and of the consequences of proceeding without it.\n2. **Interpretation.** Interpretation was provided as Title VI requires, or the person waived it in the form Title VI requires.\n3. **Notice.** Notice was actually received as \u00a7 901 requires.\n4. **Timeliness.** The decision was made within the periods of \u00a7 711, or within an extension recorded under \u00a7 711(c) or (d).\n5. **Review.** The person had available the review provided by Title X.\n\n**(b) Appointment alone is not enough.** Paragraph (a)(1) is satisfied only where counsel **appeared and participated**. An order entered in a proceeding at which appointed counsel did not appear is not a qualified order, **and no inquiry into the quality of counsel's performance is required or permitted** by this subsection \u2014 the question is appearance and participation, which the record shows.\n\n**(c) The Government bears the burden.** In any proceeding in which the status of an order is contested, **the Government bears the burden of establishing that an order is qualified.** An order is not qualified where the record is silent as to any element of subsection (a).\n\n**(d) Determined on the record.** Whether an order is qualified is determined from the record of the proceeding, **de novo**, under \u00a7 1003(b).\n\n**(e) Application in time.** An order entered **before** the effective date of Titles V through X is not a qualified order, **and no consequence whatever follows from that fact.** This Title reaches only orders entered in proceedings to which those Titles applied, and **it neither disturbs nor validates any order entered before them.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE XI \u2014 FINALITY","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1102","heading":"\u00a7 1102. Execution","part":"TITLE XI \u2014 FINALITY","body":"**(a) A qualified order is executed.** A qualified order shall be executed promptly, and not later than the period set in the schedule to this Act after it becomes final.\n\n**(b) Time to seek review.** A qualified order shall not be executed before the period for filing a petition for review has run.\n\n**(c) A timely petition.** Where a petition for review is filed within that period, execution is **stayed for 30 days** to permit an application for a stay to be made and decided. **Thereafter the ordinary standard governs**, and this subsection creates no further stay and no presumption for or against one.\n\n**(d) Orders that are not qualified.** Subsection (a) does not apply to an order that is not a qualified order. **Nothing in this Title authorizes, requires, or accelerates the execution of such an order**, and the law governing it is unchanged."},{"anchor":"sec-1103","heading":"\u00a7 1103. Successive claims","part":"TITLE XI \u2014 FINALITY","body":"**(a) Limit.** A person subject to a **qualified order** may not file a successive application for protection under section 1158 of title 8, section 1231(b)(3) of that title, or the Convention Against Torture, except on a showing of \u2014\n\n1. a **change in country conditions** material to the claim and arising after the prior decision; or\n2. **evidence material to the claim that was not available and could not through reasonable diligence have been obtained** before the prior decision.\n\n**(b) Not a merits test.** A showing under subsection (a) is made by identifying the changed condition or the new evidence and its materiality. **It is not an assessment of whether the claim will succeed**, and the Court shall not weigh the ultimate merits in deciding whether the limit applies.\n\n**(c) Non-refoulement is not limited.** Nothing in this Section permits the removal of a person to a country where the person would face persecution or torture. **A claim that removal to a particular country would violate section 1231(b)(3) or the Convention Against Torture is not a successive claim within subsection (a)** where it concerns a country **to which removal is proposed** and which was not addressed in the prior decision. A claim concerning a country to which removal is not proposed is not within this subsection.\n\n**(d) Where the order is not qualified.** Subsection (a) does not apply, and the existing limits on successive applications govern."},{"anchor":"sec-1104","heading":"\u00a7 1104. What remains open regardless","part":"TITLE XI \u2014 FINALITY","body":"**Nothing in this Title limits, and no qualified order affects \u2014**\n\n1. a motion under \u00a7 505(b), \u00a7 605(b), \u00a7 904, or section 1229a(b)(5)(C) of title 8, **concerning counsel, interpretation, or notice**;\n2. relief available on a showing of fraud, coercion, or a material misrepresentation by any officer or employee of the United States;\n3. an application for relief that becomes available for the first time after the order, including by change in law or in the person's circumstances;\n4. any protection against removal to a particular country under \u00a7 1103(c); or\n5. **any claim that the order is not a qualified order.**"},{"anchor":"sec-1105","heading":"\u00a7 1105. Construction","part":"TITLE XI \u2014 FINALITY","body":"**(a) One direction only.** This Title **adds a consequence to a proceeding that met this Act's standards.** It removes no right, shortens no period, and creates no ground of removal. **Where the standards were not met, this Title does nothing at all.**\n\n**(b) Not a presumption.** The existence of a qualified order is not evidence on any question other than those this Title addresses, and **the fact that an order is not qualified is not evidence that any provision of this Act was violated** \u2014 it means only that the record does not show it was satisfied.\n\n**(c) Severability.** \u00a7 1102 and \u00a7 1103 are severable from one another. **If \u00a7 1101 is held invalid, neither \u00a7 1102 nor \u00a7 1103 operates**, and the law governing execution and successive applications is unchanged."},{"anchor":"sec-1201","heading":"\u00a7 1201. Defenses and construction","part":"TITLE XII \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Defenses exclusive.** The defenses, immunities, exceptions, and limitations available in an action under this Act are those and only those expressly stated in it. **No defense is to be inferred from the omission of any subject from this Act**, and the absence of a provision addressing a defense recognized at common law is a determination by Congress that it is not available.\n\n**(b) No reconstitution.** A limitation omitted from this Act may not be reintroduced as an element of a plaintiff's case, a pleading requirement, a standard of proof, or a prudential limitation on jurisdiction.\n\n**(c) No channeling.** No administrative remedy is a prerequisite to an action under this Act, and **no statute establishing a remedial scheme shall be construed to channel, preclude, or displace a claim under this Act unless it so provides by express reference to this Act.**\n\n**(d) Review preserved.** No provision of this chapter limiting review of a discretionary determination shall be construed to preclude review of **whether a determination required by this Act was made, by whom, on what stated basis, and whether it satisfies the requirements this Act imposes.**\n\n**(e) Mootness.** The removal, release, or transfer of a person after the commencement of an action does not moot it where the government remains free to repeat the conduct. **The government bears the burden of establishing that it cannot reasonably be expected to recur.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE XII \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1202","heading":"\u00a7 1202. Anti-substitution","part":"TITLE XII \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"Where an authority relied upon to remove, detain, or exclude a person **is held unavailable or is determined not to confer the power asserted, and another authority is invoked to achieve substantially the same result with respect to the same person**, the conditions of this Act apply to the substituted authority according to their terms. An officer invoking a substituted authority within **180 days** shall publish a statement identifying the authority previously relied upon, the holding, the authority now invoked, and **the material differences in the conditions each imposes.**"},{"anchor":"sec-1203","heading":"\u00a7 1203. Severability","part":"TITLE XII \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"Each Title is severable from each other Title and each section from each other section. **Congress would have enacted Title I independently of Titles II through IV**, and \u00a7 101 independently of \u00a7\u00a7 102 through 104."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"1. **Term of a judge of the United States Immigration Court** (\u00a7 703(b)): [15 years]\n2. **Term of the Chief Judge** (\u00a7 702(c)): [___ years]\n3. **Period of detention after which a hearing under \u00a7 303 is required** (\u00a7 303(a)): [___ months]\n4. **Interval between subsequent hearings** (\u00a7 303(a)): [___ months]\n5. **Period after a final order after which \u00a7 304 applies** (\u00a7 304(a)): [___ months]\n6. **Period after entry within which expedited removal may apply** (\u00a7 801, amending \u00a7 1225(b)(1)(A)(iii)(II)): [___ days]\n7. **Period within which a motion under \u00a7 505(b) or \u00a7 605(b) must be filed** (\u00a7\u00a7 505(b), 605(b)(3)): [___ days after the order, reinstatement, withdrawal, or permission to depart, as applicable, or after the applicable effective date in item 11, whichever is later]\n8. **Civil penalty for a knowingly false or frivolous filing** (\u00a7 509(b)): [$___ per filing]\n9. **Period within which a qualified order shall be executed** (\u00a7 1102(a)): [___ days after it becomes final]\n10. **Minimum period a person must be afforded to obtain counsel before the hearing on the merits, where the person was not entitled to appointed counsel** (\u00a7 1101(a)(1)): [___ days]\n11. **Effective date of Title V and of the written-translation, order-condition, recording, publication, audit, and enforcement provisions of Title VI** (\u00a7\u00a7 508(e), 606(f)(2)): [___, not earlier than one year after enactment]\n12. **Share of claims decided after the applicable period that constitutes a pattern** (\u00a7 711(f-1)): [___ percent in a year]\n13. **Share of non-conforming hearings that requires an interpreter-staffing statement to Congress** (\u00a7 607(c)): [___ percent in a year]"}],"asOf":"2026-11-11"}}}};
